export const skillDocuments: Record<string, { name: string; description: string; body: string }> = {
  "clipping/SKILL.md": {
    "name": "clipping",
    "description": "Turns a long stream, VOD or podcast into separate 15 to 60 second shorts, one timeline tab per short. Read it for \"clip this\", \"make clips\", \"turn this into shorts\", \"find the best moments\", or a Twitch, Kick or YouTube link with a short-form ask.",
    "body": "# Clipping long sources into shorts\n\nA good batch is a few distinct, self-contained shorts, each opening on its hook and ending after its payoff, with every claim backed by saved transcript or observed video.\nEach short lives in its own titled tab of one shared project, keeps the source framing and audio, and is closed out with cheap data checks rather than heavy QA.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Read page moments, then call findHighlights on the shared source.\n2. Pick the evidence path by the requested content, not by voice presence:\n   - Podcast, interview, talk or requested stream dialogue: speech-led (speechLed:true), judged from the saved transcript.\n   - Gaming, sports and other action: visual events judged from short inspectVideo windows; commentary over the action does not make the visuals irrelevant.\n   - Silent content: bounded video discovery across the source, with honest inspected coverage.\n3. Rank candidates and register them with planClips (page moments).\n4. Read page batching, then build one populated tab per accepted candidate.\n5. Reframe (page framing) only when the user requests vertical reframing or a speaker crop; add finishing (page finishing) only when requested or part of the selected style.\n6. Call finishClipping, then finish. Export only when the user asks.\n\n## Defaults\n| Setting | Default |\n|---|---|\n| Short length | 15–60 s |\n| Shorts per source | min(10, qualifying distinct moments, max(3, round(sourceSeconds / 150))), within free tab slots |\n| Qualifying score | 65/100 or higher (rubric in page moments) |\n| Framing and aspect | Source framing and aspect |\n| Audio | Original audio at 1×, original loudness |\n| QA | finishClipping's inline data checks only |\n\n## Rules\n- Change framing, aspect or loudness only when the user asks, for every kind of source, gaming and action included.\n- Heavy QA is optional for every kind of clipping: use checkClip only for the user's stated concern.\n- Do not run checkPacing, 10 fps windows or getFrames sweeps on finished shorts.\n- Never claim a skipped check passed or that an unsupported action was observed.\n- Finding moments alone is an inspection request: return the ranked candidates without changing timelines.\n- A small follow-up edit to a finished short (caption, trim, style) starts with writing-scenes page edit-lanes and uses its local checks where they apply; do not repeat whole-source discovery for it.\n\n## Example\n\"Clip this\" on a 45-minute podcast:\n- Speech-led, so the moments come from the saved transcript with no video judging.\n- 14 candidates score 65 or higher. The count is min(10, 14, max(3, round(2700 / 150) = 18)) = 10 shorts.\n- planClips accepts those 10 and drops every other returned candidate with a reason (\"same payoff as H3\", \"needs prior context\").\n- Each short gets its own titled tab starting at 0, in the source framing with the original audio.\n- finishClipping, then finish. No export until the user asks.\n\n## Pages\n- moments: discover, rank and register candidates; choose clean 15–60 s boundaries.\n- batching: one project and shared source, one titled tab per short, exports on request.\n- framing: facecam-over-gameplay stacks and speaker reframing in 9:16, only when requested or needed.\n- finishing: optional captions, pacing, sound and niche styles, with the clipper caption defaults.\n"
  },
  "clipping/references/batching.md": {
    "name": "batching",
    "description": "One project and shared source analysis, one titled timeline tab per short, separate exports only when requested.",
    "body": "# A batch of separate shorts\n\nOne source, one project, shared assets and saved analysis; one titled tab per accepted short, each starting at timeline 0.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Find the project. Reuse the current project when it contains the source; on MCP call getProject and listTimelines first, and createProject only if a project is needed.\n2. Bring a missing source in once: uploadMedia/finishUpload, or importVideoLink for YouTube, Twitch VOD, Kick recording or public MP4 links.\n3. Persist the accepted list with planClips: candidate ID, title, source range and, once known, the resulting timelineId.\n4. For each accepted candidate, createTimeline from blank with the short's title as its name, using the aspect ratio findHighlights returned.\n5. Add the chosen range with addMedia from the shared asset at start 0, then inspect the new clip IDs before any reframing or polish.\n6. Build every accepted tab, then call finishClipping and finish, and report its finalMessage.\n7. Export only when asked: exportVideo once per finished tab with its timelineId, poll getExport for each job, and label each download by title.\n\n## Rules\nSources:\n- Never create a project, upload or transcribe the same source once per short.\n- If a provider blocks a recording, ask for an upload or a direct MP4 link.\n- Never claim an unsupported or live stream was imported.\n\nTabs:\n- One tab holds one short. Do not substitute a montage in one tab, or one project per short.\n- On retries, inspect existing tabs and resume completed work instead of duplicating it.\n- Use openTimeline to revisit an existing short when the editing surface needs it.\n- Preserve unrelated tabs and edits.\n- On MCP, pass projectId and the intended timelineId explicitly to every editing and inspection tool, including getFrames, so another session switching tabs cannot redirect work.\n- Timeline tabs, imports and exports work the same in MCP and the in-app turn.\n\nClose-out:\n- A failed optional check is a real finding: fix the affected tab or report it. Do not hide it or rerender unrelated passing tabs.\n- Report checkClip's sampled scope honestly.\n- Never export the open tab repeatedly assuming it selects different shorts.\n- An optional overview may list title, source range, duration, score and tab or export link. Do not wait for approval of it before building requested shorts.\n\n## Example\nFor accepted candidate H2 (source 812.4–851.0 s, title \"The upload stalled\"):\n\n```\ncreateTimeline { from: \"blank\", name: \"The upload stalled\", aspectRatio: \"landscape\" }\naddMedia { assetId: \"<shared source>\", sourceStart: 812.4, duration: 38.6, start: 0 }\ninspectTimeline\n```\n"
  },
  "clipping/references/finishing.md": {
    "name": "finishing",
    "description": "Optional captions in the clipper style, pacing, sound, loudness, hook text and niche styles for finished shorts, with restrained defaults.",
    "body": "# Optional finish\n\nA finished short is a hook-to-payoff cut that keeps the clipping defaults, plus only the embellishments the user asked for or the selected style calls for.\nFollow the user's explicit choices or selected style. Captions, dead-air removal, speed-ups, zoom punches, hit effects, light music and hook text are optional: never force them into every short or let choosing them block delivery.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Finalize timing first (pacing, cuts), so captions and overlays stay in sync.\n2. Add captions with addSubtitlesFromTranscript from saved word timings, then any hook text.\n3. Settle the mix: fades, ducking, volume balance.\n4. Normalize loudness last, and only on an explicit request.\n5. Check the final length is still 15–60 s.\n\n## Defaults\nClip-lane captions (the clipper style addSubtitlesFromTranscript creates in this lane):\n\n| Property | Default |\n|---|---|\n| Background | None |\n| Font | Montserrat Black, uppercase |\n| Color | White with a bold black outline |\n| Words per caption | 1–3 |\n| Motion | Each word pops in as spoken (karaoke template, karaokeStyle pop) |\n| Spoken word | Highlighted green, #22C55E |\n\nAn explicit user caption style wins: pass style minimal, or restyle with styleCaptions, setTextStyle or configureTextTemplate.\n\nLoudness target, when the user asks for normalization or better audio: -14 LUFS integrated, at most -1 dBTP, on the whole timeline mix of each finished tab.\n\n## Rules\nCaptions and hook text:\n- For caption and hook-text styling, read skill footage-editing page text-styling.\n- Hook text comes from the actual moment, without invented claims.\n- Keep text clear of faces, essential gameplay and platform controls.\n\nPacing:\n- For speech cleanup, read skill footage-editing; use configureSmoothZoom for zooms.\n- Preserve comedic pauses, reactions and intelligible speech.\n\nSound:\n- Keep speech and important game or room sound intelligible.\n- Inspect the mix with getSound or checkSound and balance it with setVolume. Added music (start, length, ending, ducking under speech) follows skill `music`.\n- Normalize the whole tab mix, music and effects included; target one linked sound clip only when that is the intent.\n- Respect an explicit request to keep the original loudness, removing any existing normalization.\n\nNiche styles, applied only when selected and only for their useful embellishments:\n- Gaming: the facecam over gameplay stack and the real reaction.\n- Podcast: clear speaker turns and intact thoughts.\n- IRL: stable subject framing and situational context.\n- Sports: the action and its outcome, with essential ball, player and score context visible.\n"
  },
  "clipping/references/framing.md": {
    "name": "framing",
    "description": "Gaming facecam above gameplay, mirror blur fill for a wide clip, or speaker-led podcast and IRL reframing in 9:16, using measured existing tools.",
    "body": "# Vertical framing\n\nGood framing keeps faces, the facecam and the key action in view without stretching, guessing coordinates or claiming confidence the analysis does not have.\nOrdinary podcast and talk shorts keep the source view: use fitClip fit \"contain\" if needed to keep the full picture, and do not auto-track, crop or analyze faces. Existing user framing stays intact.\n\n## Defaults\nApply this page only to gaming or action sources and to explicit visual reframing requests.\n\n| Situation | Default |\n|---|---|\n| Requested vertical reframing | 9:16 canvas, fitClip fit \"cover\" on every single-view video |\n| User wants the complete wide frame | fitClip fit \"contain\" (leaves large empty bars on a vertical short) |\n| Complete wide frame without empty bars | Mirror blur fill: the clip sharp at full width, mirrored blurred copies above and below |\n| Confident facecam overlay | Two rows: creator on top, gameplay below |\n| Creator-only or gameplay-only | One source filling the frame with the supported crop |\n| Uncertain or unbordered layout | followSubject mode \"face\", or a stable wider view if no face is clear |\n| Fixed single speaker | One measured stable crop |\n| Co-visible speakers exchanging turns | fitClip \"cover\", then followSubject mode \"active-speaker\" |\n\n## Gaming: creator above gameplay\nWorkflow:\n1. Run detectFacecam once per source asset.\n2. Inspect the saved facecam samples inside the retained range. If the layout kind or boxes change, split the video at those confirmed source times.\n3. Make two synchronized video instances from the same asset and source range on separate V layers (duplicateClips), keeping one audible source track.\n4. Call applyLayout two-rows with detectedRegions:true, creator clips on top and gameplay clips below.\n5. Use cropClip only for a measured refinement.\n\nRules:\n- A face rectangle alone is not the webcam panel; never invent a panel from a face box.\n- Read frames at retained-range boundaries and layout changes: detection samples cannot prove the layout between them.\n- Never apply the layout across a known layout change; split first.\n- Resolve a refused layout on the split sections before finishing. Do not delete one row and silently abandon the failed layout.\n- For creator-only or gameplay-only sources, do not build a duplicate empty row.\n- For an uncertain layout, inspect the affected source range before choosing followSubject or a wider view.\n- Never report a low-confidence panel as certain.\n\n## Mirror blur fill\nUse it when the whole wide picture must stay visible on a taller canvas (9:16, 4:5, 1:1) and empty bars would look unfinished.\nWorkflow:\n1. Set the target aspect ratio.\n2. Call applyLayout mirror-blur with the exact clip IDs that stay sharp. It adds the background copies itself; do not duplicate clips first.\n3. Read one frame per clip with getFrames to confirm no bars remain.\n\nRules:\n- Only for a source wider than the canvas; a source that already fills the height needs fitClip instead.\n- Finish trims, splits and moves first. To change a clip later, delete its copies and apply the layout again.\n\n## Speaker reframing and visual IRL cuts\nWorkflow:\n1. For a batch with co-visible speakers, call analyzeMedia mode \"speakers\" for each accepted source window before changing tabs, at most two calls at a time. Skip this for fixed single-speaker shots.\n2. In each tab, apply the default for the shot type from the Defaults table.\n3. Read the per-short framing receipt and the saved framing intervals around each switch.\n\nRules:\n- Do not run mouth matching on a single speaker who never changes.\n- Reuse saved framing when its coverage includes the short; never analyze the whole source per tab.\n- If the crop follows the wrong person, turn following off and keep a wider view.\n- Report fallback seconds explicitly.\n- Speaker IDs and faceIds from separate analysis windows are not the same people; never equate numeric voice IDs with real-world names.\n\n## Measuring and QA\n- Use source readFrames only while measuring framing: panel edges, layout changes and active-speaker crops, after planClips accepts candidates.\n- Reuse saved analysis across every short.\n- Requested framing QA on a finished tab uses only the checkClip framing sheet. Glance once for face or facecam crop, mouths, key action and text visibility.\n- Fix an affected crop and rerun checkClip. Report sampled coverage and fallback seconds; never check finished shorts frame by frame.\n\n## Example\nConfident overlay, retained range already split at layout changes:\n\n```\ndetectFacecam { assetId: \"<source>\" }\nduplicateClips { clipIds: [\"<video>\"] }            → new video V2 + audio A2\nsetVolume { clipId: \"<A2>\", volume: 0 }\napplyLayout { layout: \"two-rows\", detectedRegions: true,\n              topClipIds: [\"<V2>\"], bottomClipIds: [\"<video>\"] }\n```\n"
  },
  "clipping/references/moments.md": {
    "name": "moments",
    "description": "Scans the whole source, ranks evidenced hooks and payoffs, and chooses 15 to 60 second boundaries.",
    "body": "# Finding moments for clipping\n\nGood candidates are distinct, self-contained moments with a hook in the first seconds and a resolved payoff, each backed by cited source times.\nUse this search only for a clipping request.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Speech-led sources: read the saved timed transcript once and propose distinct, self-contained hooks and payoffs. Register them with planClips found, quoting exact words and times. Spoken moments need no video judging or frames.\n2. Action sources: propose timestamped candidates from the findHighlights evidence. Call inspectVideo with a focused question only where visual context matters; start with short windows and extend only a specific uncertain event. Reuse saved observations.\n3. Silent sources: sample a bounded set of short video windows across the source, refine the observed events, and disclose the inspected coverage.\n4. Score each candidate with the rubric below and pick the count from the clipping Defaults.\n5. Call planClips before building: accept the chosen candidates and drop every other returned top-10 candidate with a concrete reason.\n6. When shorts are requested, carry the ranked plan into page batching.\n\n## Defaults\nViral-potential score out of 100:\n\n| Criterion | 0 | Middle | Full |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hook (25) | absent | 12: after setup | 25: in the first 2 s |\n| Payoff (25) | absent | 12: implied | 25: resolved |\n| Clarity (20) | prior short required | 10: one missing detail | 20: self-contained |\n| Surprise (20) | absent | 10: stated | 20: visible reaction |\n| Quotability (10) | absent | 5: context-dependent | 10: standalone line |\n\nCount examples: 8:16 source with 4 good moments → 3; 45 min with 14 → 10; 8:16 with 2 → 2, naming the reason no third qualified.\n\n## Rules\nEvidence:\n- Stills and scene cuts cannot establish continuous action.\n- For silent content, never invent transcript-based rankings or claim coverage of unseen source.\n- Cite source times for every candidate. Scores are editorial estimates, never promised views.\n- Keep uncertainty visible; never fill a requested count with unsupported highlights.\n\nChoosing:\n- Prefer distinct moments over overlapping versions of the same joke.\n- Never pad. State any shortfall. When fewer than the target qualify and dropped reasons do not explain it, give planClips a concrete shortfallReason, for example \"Other fights repeat the same payoff or need setup beyond 60 s.\"\n- Account for strong moments left out only because of the requested count, too. Good drop reasons: \"same payoff as H3\", \"needs prior context\", \"lower-ranked than the three requested\".\n- The accepted name becomes the short's exact, distinct tab title.\n- For gaming and sports, record critical HUD regions in the plan when they matter.\n- If review drops a candidate that is already built, open a retained or original tab first, then update planClips with the reason.\n\nBoundaries:\n- Keep planClips' clean in and out points, or improve them with word timings while keeping the grounded hook and payoff.\n- Read exact word timings around speech boundaries from the saved transcript in short source windows.\n- The first sentence names its subject: for \"That's why it failed\", also retain \"The upload stalled.\"\n- End after the payoff and its real reaction, before the reset. Keep words and the thought complete.\n- If a candidate cannot stand alone within 60 s, choose another or explain the constraint.\n- Respect exact user timestamps and flag any conflict with the 15–60 s range.\n"
  },
  "footage-editing/SKILL.md": {
    "name": "footage-editing",
    "description": "Edits the user's footage and audio (talking heads, vlogs, podcasts, reels, screen recordings): cuts, speech cleanup, captions, b-roll, layers, transitions and effects. Read before editing footage or audio; small fixes to an existing edit start with writing-scenes page edit-lanes.",
    "body": "# Editing footage\n\nA good footage edit keeps every retained word intact and in sync, removes only what the user asked to remove, and leaves unrelated clips, approved work and existing styling untouched. Decisions rest on saved evidence (transcript, audio, frames), never on guesses.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Run inspectTimeline for current clip IDs, layers and approved work. Edit around approved clips.\n2. For a plain talking-head edit in the reel lane, follow the reel skill. For a small fix to an existing edit, follow writing-scenes page `edit-lanes`.\n3. Read only the evidence the request needs: the saved transcript for speech edits, frames for visual edits (page `speech`).\n4. Make the core edit in as few calls as possible: one retainSourceRanges or cleanSpeech call for speech, exact clipIds batches for identical changes.\n5. Add captions after the final cuts (page `text-styling`).\n6. Add effects only when the user asked for them or authorized creative styling (page `layers-and-effects`).\n7. Check the result with inspectTimeline (duration, gaps, linked audio, caption sync), then run the checks writing-scenes page `edit-lanes` gives for the lane.\n8. Report what changed, including the speechCleanup summary in one short sentence (for example: \"Removed 74 pauses and 3 filler words, 12.4 s.\").\n\n## Defaults\n| Setting | Default |\n| --- | --- |\n| New captions (all lanes except clip) | Minimal, readable, static text, no background; subtle outline or shadow allowed |\n| New captions (clip lane) | Clipper captions from addSubtitlesFromTranscript (clipping page `finishing`) |\n| Speech cuts | retainSourceRanges with tightenSilence:true |\n| Transcript reads | SOURCE windows of at most 30 s |\n| J/L cut offset | 0.1–0.2 s, only where it helps |\n| Tracks | V 1–24 (video, images, text, solids), A 1–12 (audio), FX 1–24 (effects only) |\n\n## Rules\n- Ordinary trims, moves and splits never authorize decoration such as effects, music or punch-ins.\n- Any song in the edit (where it starts, cutting to it, length, ending, ducking) follows skill `music`.\n- An explicit user request (style, timestamps, keeping pauses, no captions) overrides every default here.\n- Defaults apply to new work only; never restyle existing captions or clips unasked.\n- Identify user-named footage from inspected project references; never claim a source or object is absent without inspection evidence.\n- Use saved analysis that exists; request only the missing evidence, and run addSubtitlesFromTranscript, useStrongestMoment or alignCutsToBeat only after their analysis exists.\n- When a cut boundary is uncertain, keep the footage rather than spending the run rechecking every boundary.\n- For rough-cut cleanup plus captions, make the core cuts and add the captions before any optional repeated visual checks.\n\n## Example\n\"Cut the pauses and add captions\" on a 3-minute talking head, outside the clip lane:\n\n```\ninspectTimeline                         → clip v1, no approved work\ncleanSpeech { clipId: \"v1\" }            → speechCleanup summary from the saved transcript\naddSubtitlesFromTranscript { assetId }  → minimal captions, no background\ninspectTimeline                         → duration, no gaps, captions in sync\n```\n\nReply: \"Removed 74 pauses and 3 filler words, 12.4 s, and added captions.\"\n\n## Pages\n- speech: transcript reading, speech cuts and cleanup, J/L cuts, and when to look at the picture.\n- text-styling: caption and title defaults, styling tools, placement, and the full text catalog.\n- layers-and-effects: tracks and layers, b-roll, undo and revisions, speed, and effect planning rules. Transitions: the `transitions` skill.\n"
  },
  "footage-editing/references/layers-and-effects.md": {
    "name": "layers-and-effects",
    "description": "Layer, b-roll, undo, speed and effect judgment for footage edits, plus the effect planning rules. Read before arranging layers, adding b-roll, changing speed, or adding any effect; transitions live in the transitions skill.",
    "body": "# Layers and effects\n\nA good result changes only the requested clips, keeps linked video and audio together, and uses each effect for a reason the viewer can see. Exact parameters live in each tool's description; this page covers when to use what.\n\n## Rules\n- Batch identical changes across clips with the tool's clipIds selector in one call; run independent calls with known inputs in the same step.\n- Use moveClips and resizeClips for a selected group, reorderLayer for a whole layer, and move or trim for one clip.\n- Never delete an occupied track's clips just to remove its row.\n- To return to an earlier version: writing-scenes page `edit-lanes` (Direction and feedback).\n- Unlink a video/audio pair only when the user wants to keep one side while editing the other.\n- Footage and sound fades are clip settings (configureVisualFade, configureAudio), never separate FX clips.\n- After setPlaybackRate or setSpeedRamp, read the new clip duration before any further timing edit.\n- Transitions (hard cut, whips, fades, addTransition settings): read the `transitions` skill.\n- When a request needs an unsupported capability (animating an object inside a video, a global camera, automatic parenting between layers, or generated footage), say so instead of approximating it.\n\n{{revline:0}}\n"
  },
  "footage-editing/references/speech.md": {
    "name": "speech",
    "description": "Speech edits from saved transcript and audio evidence: removing pauses, fillers, false starts and retakes, assembling takes, J/L cuts, and when an edit needs the picture. Read before any speech cut or cleanup.",
    "body": "# Speech edits\n\nA good speech edit keeps every retained sentence complete and natural, cuts only between words, and stays in sync with its linked audio and captions. Speech edits are decided from the transcript and audio, not by watching the footage.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Read the saved transcript around the affected SOURCE range with inspectAssetAnalysis. Pass every needed sentence window in one call.\n2. Choose the edit:\n   - A whole clip (\"remove the ums\", \"cut the pauses\", \"clean up the retakes\"): call cleanSpeech once.\n   - Chosen takes or several cuts in one clip: collect the clean, complete-sentence SOURCE ranges and call retainSourceRanges once with tightenSilence:true.\n   - A quick local edit: isolate the affected speech first, so cleanup leaves unrelated speech unchanged.\n3. Join the pieces: closeGaps for footage with no timed overlays; for layered edits, follow writing-scenes page `edit-lanes` to keep overlays in sync.\n\n## Rules\n- Trimming, cutting, choosing takes and removing repeats, fillers or pauses are transcript-and-audio edits: never call inspectVideo or inspectFrames for them.\n- Look at the picture only when the request asks for a visual judgement, such as eyes-to-camera cuts, awkward look-ups or script glances. Then read the visual-inspection skill first.\n- A short-form deliverable alone does not justify viewing the footage.\n- Keep natural pauses (keepPauses:true) only when the user asks for them; exact user timestamps are cut without tightening.\n- Use saved shot boundaries for existing cut points and transcript word timings for speech cuts.\n- Request word-timed transcription when the user needs captions or edits at specific spoken words; request beat analysis only when the edit needs measured music timing.\n- For a new visual question (a fight, a reaction, an object), call inspectVideo with the user's actual question and an optional source window. A saved overview does not answer it.\n- Never invent unseen events or spoken words.\n- Listen only to an uncertain short join or an actual audio problem.\n\n## J and L cuts\n- Use setSplitEdits selectively, where source handles and inspected context (b-roll, a reaction shot) make a speech join smoother.\n- Keep visible speaking mouths in sync: a same-camera talking-head jump cut stays a straight cut.\n- Never apply J/L to every cut or alternate them mechanically.\n- Batch all chosen edges in one call after inspectTimeline.\n- Keep speech audio and caption timing unchanged; never repeat words or fabricate room tone.\n- Follow the user's explicit cut preferences.\n"
  },
  "footage-editing/references/text-styling.md": {
    "name": "text-styling",
    "description": "Caption and title styling for footage edits: the default caption look, which tool changes what, placement, and the full catalog of fonts, looks, animations and text controls. Read before adding, styling or animating any caption or title.",
    "body": "# Text styling\n\nGood text is readable at a glance, clear of faces and app controls, and styled the same way for each role (title, captions, callout).\n\n## Workflow\n1. New captions: run addSubtitlesFromTranscript after the final cuts. Their default look per lane is in the footage-editing Defaults.\n2. Existing text: run inspectClip first, then change only the requested words or controls. Caption text or timing fixes follow writing-scenes page `edit-lanes`, which also sets the frame checks.\n3. Style in one call per change: batch the same appearance across exact clipIds.\n\n## Defaults\n| Change | Tool |\n| --- | --- |\n| Font, size, solid colors, position, rotation, blend, text case | styleCaptions (many clips) or styleTextClip (one) |\n| Stroke, shadow, glow | setTextStyle |\n| A named editable look | applyTextLook |\n| Animation, including Word reveal and Pop-in words | setTextAnimation |\n| Highlight or accent color of a template | configureTextTemplate |\n| Copy one caption's look to others | applyCaptionStyle |\n| Caption start/end | setCaptionTiming |\n| Words | editText |\n\n## Rules\n- Change animation with setTextAnimation only when the user asks for an animation change.\n- Vertical captions stay out of the bottom 20% of the frame, where app controls sit, and at least 24 design px from faces. Check the extremes of any movement with getFrames.\n- Use one look per text role: title, captions, callout.\n\n## Catalog\nEvery font, look, animation, template and text control, with guidance:\n\n```json\n{{revline:0}}\n```\n"
  },
  "genesis/SKILL.md": {
    "name": "genesis",
    "description": "Genesis (MCP only): Meta SAM 3.1 subject layers and VDA-Small depth passes, reused by every function: colour only a subject, grade, blur, glow, outline, title behind, relight (LightX), portrait blur, focus pull, fog and depth scan. Read before segment, layerEffect, textBehind, depth, depthEffect or relight.",
    "body": "# Genesis\n\nGenesis builds on two reusable passes per clip: a SAM 3.1 **layer** per subject (`segment`) and one **depth pass** (`depth`). Every function reads them; neither is ever run twice.\n\n| Want | Pass | Function | Page |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Colour, grade, blur, glow or outline one subject; title behind it | layer | `layerEffect`, `textBehind` | this page |\n| Change the light (whole frame or one subject) | (layer + depth) | `relight` | **relight** |\n| Portrait blur, focus pull, fog/haze, depth scan | depth (+ layer) | `depthEffect` | **depth** |\n| Fake camera move on a still moment | none | `freezeFrameParallax` (not Genesis) | — |\n\nRead page relight before `relight` and page depth before `depth` or `depthEffect` (`readSkill {name:\"genesis\", page:\"relight\"}`).\n\n## Layers\nOne `segment` call makes one **layer**: SAM 3.1 tracks the subject through the clip, and the worker saves a per-frame mask (a transparent cut-out on the source clock), the box of that mask's opaque pixels on every frame, `model: sam-3.1` and the cost. Every layer function reuses that layer. The masking is never run again.\n\n## Layer workflow\n1. `inspectTimeline` → the footage `clipId`. `readFrames` 1–3 stills → pick a frame where the subject is whole and unblocked.\n2. `segment {clipId, prompt, at}` → `layerId`. Prompt, best first:\n   - `{box:{left:.38,top:.12,right:.66,bottom:.98}}`: normalized SOURCE coordinates from the still. Most reliable.\n   - `{point:{x:.52,y:.4}}`: on the subject's torso, not an edge.\n   - `{noun:\"skateboarder\"}`: SAM 3.1 must find exactly **one** match on frame `at`. \"person\" in a crowd fails; use a box.\n   - The mask is what SAM 3.1 calls the subject. It can leave out what the subject holds: \"gardener in white\" gave the man without his raised shears. A box around both kept only part of the shears. Check the cut-out with `getFrames` before relying on a held object.\n3. `getLayer {layerId}` every 15 s until `status: \"ready\"`. Measured: 10 s of 720p at 24 fps = 237 frames, **$0.05**, 142 s. 5 s = 126 frames, **$0.03**, 133 s. `costUsd` is the real price of the frames SAM processed.\n   - `coverage.visibleFrames / coverage.frames` below 0.8 means the subject was lost. Segment again with a box at a later `at`.\n   - `boxes` lists every mask frame: `boxes.boxes[k]` is `[left, top, right, bottom]` (normalized, `null` while hidden) at SOURCE second `boxes.from + k / boxes.fps`. It always matches the cut-out's pixels. A call returns up to 720 frames; pass `from`/`to` (SOURCE seconds) for part of a layer, and continue from `boxes.next`.\n   - To make an overlay follow the subject frame-exactly, map each box to the timeline: `clip.start + (sourceSecond - clip.sourceStart) / playbackRate` of the footage clip (without a speed ramp).\n4. Apply functions (examples below) in any order. Each returns new clip IDs stacked directly above the footage, always in the order footage < colour/blur plates < titles < subject cut-outs. Example: `colorOnly` then `textBehind` gives V1 footage, V2 grey plate, V3 title, V4 subject (one subject copy, reused).\n5. `getFrames` at the moment the subject moves fastest, plus one frame either side. Use `resolution:1080` for edges. Check that the edges hug the subject (≤ 3 px halo) and nothing pops. If a batch made only of the clip's last frames fails with `zero_output`, add an earlier time (e.g. `0`) to the same call.\n6. `finish`. It checks every layer the timeline uses: through layer functions, as a clip, or as scene media. It fails until each one is ready, covers what plays, stays in sync with its footage and was checked with getFrames after your last edit.\n\n## Layer functions\n| Want | Call |\n|---|---|\n| Colour only the subject, rest black and white | `layerEffect {layerId, effect:\"colorOnly\"}` (`amount:.7` keeps 30% colour) |\n| Warm up only the subject | `layerEffect {layerId, effect:\"grade\", exposure:.2, temperature:25}` |\n| Darken the background | `layerEffect {layerId, effect:\"grade\", target:\"background\", exposure:-1.2}` |\n| Portrait background blur | `layerEffect {layerId, effect:\"blur\"}` (`amount` 0–1, default .45) |\n| Soften only the subject (dream look) | `layerEffect {layerId, effect:\"blur\", target:\"subject\", amount:.3}`. Not for hiding faces or plates: the sharp edge shows through. Use a tracked `setMask` blur for that. |\n| Neon halo | `layerEffect {layerId, effect:\"glow\", color:\"#36F9F2\", glow:28}` |\n| Gameplay player outline | `layerEffect {layerId, effect:\"outline\", color:\"#FF3B30\", width:4}` |\n| Title behind the subject | `textBehind {layerId, text:\"MONDAY\", y:.3}` |\n\n## Rules\n1. One subject per layer. Two players means two `segment` calls and two `layerEffect` calls.\n2. Masks cover the source range of the clip they were segmented from, plus 0.25 s on each side. To apply to only part of a clip, `split` the footage at the range first, then pass the piece's `clipId`.\n3. Do not trim, move or change the speed of the footage after applying. Copies are synced at apply time, and finish fails on a 1-frame drift. To retime, `undoLatestEdit` the Genesis edit, change the footage, then apply again (the layer is reused free).\n4. textBehind needs the subject to overlap the letters. For a talking head, use `y:.25–.35` and ≤ 10 characters on one line (fontSize defaults to ~90% of the width).\n5. Masks come from SAM 3.1 alone. If MODEL_API_KEY is missing on the worker, `segment` fails clearly. It never falls back to SAM2. Report it with `reportIssue`.\n6. Fast motion blur and hair give soft edges. If `getFrames` shows a halo above 3 px, use `glow` or `outline` to hide the edge, or choose a different shot.\n7. A cut-out used as scene media is checked from the scene's `<video>` timing. Write the same literal `data-start`, `data-offset` and `data-rate` on every `<video>` of that scene (e.g. `data-start=\"0\" data-offset=\"0\"`); computed or looped timing cannot be checked, and finish warns.\n8. Relight and depth effects make a new video asset; `applyJob` points the clip at it, and one `undoLatestEdit` restores the original. They stack with each other (apply one, then run the next on the clip that now shows it). Layer functions stack the original subject over the footage, so they do not combine with a relit or depth-effect clip: for \"blur or fog everything except her\", use `depthEffect` with `keepLayerId`.\n9. Not in Genesis yet: selective speed (`retimeLayer`) and tracked image replacement (`replaceSurface`). Do not fake them with masks or generative models. Say they are not available.\n"
  },
  "genesis/references/depth.md": {
    "name": "depth",
    "description": "Genesis depth pack: one VDA-Small depth pass per clip, reused by portrait blur, focus pull, fog/haze and depth scan, alone or with SAM layers. Read before depth or depthEffect.",
    "body": "# Depth pack\n\n`depth` runs Video Depth Anything (VDA-Small, Apache-2.0) once per clip and stores a temporally stable depth video, reused like a SAM layer. `depthEffect` builds a plain, viewer-obvious effect from it with Genesis's own compositor on the media worker: no model call and no generation, so it never changes what is in the frame.\n\n## Workflow\n1. `depth {clipId}` → `jobId` (the depth pass). If the effect keeps or focuses on a subject, `segment` that subject at the same time.\n2. `getJob` every 15–30 s until `ready` (and `getLayer` for any layer).\n3. `depthEffect {clipId, depthId, effect, …}` → a new `jobId`. Poll `getJob`, then `applyJob`. One `undoLatestEdit` restores the clip.\n4. `getFrames` with `resolution:1080` where the effect changes most (the end of a pull or sweep, the farthest fog), then `finish`.\n\nSeveral effects of one clip each start from the same depth pass in parallel. To stack them (fog over a relit clip, blur after fog), apply one, then run the next `depthEffect` on the clip that now shows it.\n\n## Effects\n| Effect | What the viewer sees | Call |\n|---|---|---|\n| Portrait blur | Sharp at the focus depth; blur grows with distance from it | `{effect:\"portraitBlur\", focus:{layerId}}` or `focus:{depth:.2}` / `{point:{x,y}}` |\n| Focus pull | Focus travels from one subject or depth to another over time | `{effect:\"focusPull\", from:{layerId:A}, to:{layerId:B}, start, end}` |\n| Fog / haze | Haze thickens with distance; near things stay clear | `{effect:\"fog\", amount:.5, color:\"#C8CDD2\", near:.15}` |\n| Depth scan | A glowing line sweeps through the scene by depth | `{effect:\"depthScan\", direction:\"frontToBack\", start, end, color, width}` |\n\n- `focus` / `from` / `to`: `{layerId}` follows a SAM subject's depth every frame (best for people who move); `{point:{x,y}}` reads the depth under a frame point; `{depth}` is fixed (0 nearest, 1 farthest).\n- `amount`: blur strength (.5 ≈ 24 px at 1080p at the farthest point), fog density, or scan brightness.\n- `start` / `end` are timeline seconds inside the clip (default: the whole clip). The pull eases in and out; the scan line exists only between them.\n- `keepLayerId` leaves one subject untouched: \"blur everything except the woman\" is `portraitBlur` with `focus:{layerId:W}, keepLayerId:W`; \"fog behind her but not on her\" is `fog` with `keepLayerId:W`.\n\n## When each fits\n| Request | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| \"blur the background\", \"portrait mode\", \"make him pop\" | portraitBlur on the subject's layer, with `keepLayerId` for a clean edge |\n| \"rack focus from her to him\", \"shift focus to the gardener\" | focusPull between two layers |\n| \"misty\", \"hazy morning\", \"atmosphere\" | fog |\n| \"scanner\", \"lidar sweep\", \"techy reveal\" | depthScan |\n| \"fake camera move\", \"2.5D parallax\", \"push in on a still\" | not here: use `freezeFrameParallax` |\n\nDescribe what the effect does when you propose it; do not pick a strength or colour the user did not ask for beyond the defaults.\n\n## Cost, time and limits\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Depth pass | $0.04 per second of footage (fal, VDA-Small). Measured: 8 s of 1080p at 24 fps = **$0.32**, ready in 45 s |\n| Depth effect | No model cost. Measured on 8 s of 1080p: fog 48 s, depth scan 24 s, portrait blur and focus pull 90–105 s (blur is the slow part) |\n| Max range | 60 s per depth pass or effect |\n| Output | 1080p video on the source clock, picture only (the clip keeps its sound) |\n| Reuse | One depth pass serves every effect and subject relight of that footage, free |\n\n## Rules\n1. Depth is relative (near to far within the shot), not metres. Pick focus by subject or point, not by guessing a number, unless the user gives one.\n2. Thin things (hair, branches, railings) get soft depth edges; portrait blur can halo them slightly. `keepLayerId` with a SAM layer gives the subject a clean edge.\n3. Glass, mirrors, water and the sky have unreliable depth. If fog or blur looks wrong there, say so.\n4. VDA-Small only. Base and Large are non-commercial; never ask for them.\n"
  },
  "genesis/references/relight.md": {
    "name": "relight",
    "description": "Genesis relight with LightX: whole frame or one SAM subject, named moments or an explicit sun, depth falloff, allowance and cost. Read before relight.",
    "body": "# Relight\n\n`relight` changes the light in a clip: a named moment (\"golden hour\") or an explicit sun (azimuth, elevation, colour temperature). LightX relights 49 frames sampled evenly across the clip in one fal call; Genesis carries that light change onto every original frame at 1080p, following the motion between samples. Detail, faces and text come from the original footage, so LightX never redraws them.\n\n## Workflow\n1. `inspectTimeline` → footage `clipId`. The clip must play at most **15 s** of source; trim or `split` longer clips first.\n2. Decide the scope (below). Subject only: `segment` the subject and `depth` the clip at the same time, then poll `getLayer` and `getJob` until both are ready.\n3. `relight {clipId, light, layerId?, depthId?}` → `jobId`. The reply quotes `estimatedUsd` and the month's `allowance`.\n4. `getJob {jobId}` every 30 s until `ready`. Then `applyJob {jobId}`: the clip points at the relit asset as one edit; one `undoLatestEdit` restores the original.\n5. `getFrames` with `resolution:1080` at the start, middle and end of the clip, then `finish`.\n\n## Whole frame or subject only\n| The user says | Scope | Call |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"make this golden hour\", \"relight the shot\", \"night scene\" | Whole frame | `relight {clipId, light}` |\n| \"light her face\", \"put the woman in sunset light\", \"only the car\" | Subject | `segment` + `depth`, then `relight {clipId, light, layerId, depthId}` |\n| \"brighter on him, keep the background as is\" | Subject, `falloff:0` | as above |\n| \"warm light around her that spills onto the bench\" | Subject, `falloff:.5–.8` | as above |\n\nA whole-frame relight changes the sky, ground and every person together. A subject relight keeps the scene's original light and changes only the subject, plus a spill into what sits near it in depth and in the frame (`falloff`, default .35). The depth pass is required for subject relight: without it the new light would stop at the mask edge and look pasted on.\n\n## Named moment or explicit angle\n| The user gives | Use | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| A time or mood: golden hour, sunset, noon, overcast, blue hour, moonlight, street light at night, neon, firelight, studio | `{moment, from?}` | `{moment:\"goldenHour\", from:\"left\"}` |\n| A direction or angle: \"sun from behind her, low\", \"45° from the right\", \"3200K key from above\" | `{azimuth, elevation, kelvin}` | `{azimuth:-60, elevation:20, kelvin:3500}` |\n\n- `from` is the side of the frame the light comes from (left, right, top, bottom). Each moment has a default side; pass `from` only when the user names one or the scene's existing light clearly comes from a side.\n- `azimuth` is seen from the camera: 0 = from the camera, 90 = frame right, -90 = frame left, 180 = from behind (backlight). `elevation` 0 = horizon, 90 = overhead. `kelvin` 1900 candle, 3200 tungsten/golden, 5600 daylight (default), 7500+ shade or blue.\n- `detail` adds words about the light only (\"light haze\"). Never describe the subject or scene: LightX is told only about light.\n- `strength` (default 1) blends toward the original: .5 is halfway. Use it when the user asks for \"a hint of\" or \"subtle\" light.\n\n## One by one or in parallel\n| Situation | Do |\n|---|---|\n| Several clips, same light | Start every `relight` at once, then poll each `jobId`. Jobs are independent. |\n| Several looks for one clip (\"show me golden hour and blue hour\") | Start both at once; apply the one the user picks. Each costs its own call. |\n| Subject relight on a clip that is also relit whole-frame | One by one: apply the whole-frame result first, then relight the subject on the clip that now shows it. |\n| Relight plus a depth effect (fog, blur) on the same clip | Relight first and apply it; then run `depthEffect` on the relit clip with the same depth pass. |\n| Allowance nearly used | One by one, shortest clips first; tell the user what is left. |\n\n## Cost, time and limits\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Price | $0.10 per LightX output second; one call returns 49 frames at 10 fps = **$0.49 per clip** (any length up to 15 s) |\n| Measured, whole frame (8 s, 1080p) | **$0.49**, 523 s end to end (about 7 min of it fal's queue and LightX) |\n| Measured, subject only (8 s, woman, with depth) | **$0.49** for LightX (plus the reused depth pass), 172 s end to end |\n| Allowance | 60 relight seconds a month (120 on Pro), counted in clip seconds; failed jobs do not count |\n| Max length | 15 s of source per clip |\n| Output | 1080p, the source's frame rate, picture only (the clip keeps its sound) |\n| Reuse | The same clip, light and scope reuse a finished result for free |\n\nPast the allowance `relight` refuses with the seconds used. Say so plainly and offer a shorter range; never work around it.\n\n## Rules\n1. Explicit angles run in LightX `ic` mode: the nearest side plus the angle and colour in words. LightX's `hdr` mode (an environment map) is not used: on fal it refuses non-.exr images and then cannot decode a real .exr. The reply's `mode` says which ran.\n2. Fast motion between the 49 samples can leave a soft light edge that lags a moving subject by a frame or two. Check the frame where the subject moves fastest.\n3. Relight changes light, not content. If the user wants objects, weather or the sky replaced, say Genesis does not do that.\n4. Do not trim, move or retime the clip after `applyJob` beyond the relit range; finish fails if the clip plays footage the result does not cover. `undoLatestEdit`, retime, then relight again.\n"
  },
  "gsap/SKILL.md": {
    "name": "gsap",
    "description": "Gives the engine rules for using GSAP inside Revline's frame-seeked scenes, plus GreenSock's official API pages (timeline, core, plugins, performance, utils). Read when writing GSAP code; it covers how GSAP works, while launch-films-motions decides how films look and move.",
    "body": "# GSAP in Revline scenes\n\nGSAP choreographs scene motion: staggers, overlaps, word and letter reveals, SplitText, MorphSVG, DrawSVG, motion paths. A good scene puts every move on a supplied, paused timeline, so preview, scrubbing and export draw identical frames.\n\nThis page holds no style: durations, eases and looks are decided in launch-films-motions. The API pages adapt GreenSock's MIT skills (github.com/greensock/gsap-skills) for Revline; these rules win over them.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Build the DOM and set every starting state where elements are created.\n2. Put each move on `tl` (or a section's timeline) with an explicit position.\n3. Look up API details in the GreenSock pages (Pages).\n\n## Rules\n\n### The clock\n- **Timelines are always paused and seeked to the frame**, for preview, scrubbing and export alike.\n- Never play(), restart(), reverse(), change timeScale, or rely on a move playing in real time.\n- **Every move goes on a supplied timeline:** the scene's `tl`, or the child timeline a `section(...)` callback receives (section-local seconds), always with a position parameter.\n- The motion helpers (`shot`, `push`, `hit`, `cursor`, `beat`) put their moves on the timeline you pass them; API reference: writing-scenes page `films`, Motion helpers.\n- **Whips use the `whip(tl, {...})` helper** the scene receives (launch-films-motions page `shot-changes`), not hand-written tweens.\n- Never use ScrollTrigger, ScrollSmoother, ScrollTo, Observer, Draggable, Inertia, Flip, GSDevTools, gsap.ticker, requestAnimationFrame, setTimeout or setInterval. None of them follow the frame clock.\n- Never create a move outside a seeked timeline (a bare gsap.to, gsap.from or gsap.delayedCall).\n- No responsive or reduced-motion branches: gsap.matchMedia does not apply, since the canvas has a fixed size and every frame is rendered.\n\n### Starting states\n- Starting states go where elements are built, so the first frame never flashes.\n- `tl.from` and `tl.fromTo` apply their start state when the timeline is built. Keep immediateRender for the first entrance; later from/fromTo moves on the same property set it false.\n- Anything else hidden at its first frame gets `gsap.set` (or inline style) right where it is created.\n- Never `tl.set(el, {…}, 0)` for a starting state: a set at position 0 does not apply on the first frame drawn, so the part flashes for one frame.\n\n### Properties and cost\n- Animate transforms and opacity: x, y, xPercent, yPercent, scale, rotation and opacity or autoAlpha. Use transforms for movement. will-change is unnecessary.\n- **Blur budget at 1080p:** blur at most 8 elements for at most 0.5 s at a time. Avoid full-frame blur except in measured copies and whips (launch-films-motions page `shot-changes`).\n\n### Callbacks, randomness, steps and loops\n- **Callbacks only write derived values:** onUpdate may write a counter's text. Nothing may depend on how often or in which order callbacks fire, since seeking jumps.\n- **Randomness is seeded:** Math.random, gsap.utils.random and shuffle give the same values in preview and export when called while the timeline is built. Never call them per frame or inside onUpdate.\n- **Instant changes:** `tl.set` at an explicit time, or `ease: 'steps(n)'`.\n- **Loops:** `repeat: -1` (with `yoyo: true` for back-and-forth) on a move inside the timeline.\n\n### Plugins\n- {{revline:0}} and EasePack are loaded and registered. Import them from `gsap/<Name>`; registering again is harmless. No other plugin loads.\n\n## Example\n```ts\nconst card = root.querySelector('.card');\ngsap.set(card, { autoAlpha: 0 });                      // hidden from the first frame\ntl.to(card, { autoAlpha: 1, y: 0, duration: .35, ease: 'power3.out' }, 1.2)\n  .to(card, { autoAlpha: 0, duration: .28, ease: 'power2.in' }, 3.4);\n```\n\n## Text functions\nThe scene function receives `reveal`, `exit`, `swap`, `counter`, `type` and `mark` beside `tl` and `section`. Each builds its moves while the scene builds and returns its own timeline, placed at `at` (seconds, rounded to a whole frame) on the timeline being built: inside a `section(...)` build that section's, otherwise the scene's `tl`. Pass `tl` to place it elsewhere. `el` is an element already appended to the scene, or a selector inside `root`.\n\nUnits: distances are em of the element's font size (`'24px'` strings also work); `frames` and `stagger` are whole frames. Every value is clamped to its range; a property left out or set to `false` does not move. Defaults and ranges are stored as time and converted at the film's fps; the frame counts below are at 60 fps. Sources are launch films measured frame by frame (film and frames given).\n\nThey keep these mechanics: splits wait for the scene's fonts and keep the measured kerning, line breaks and layout, so nothing moves when text splits; ligatures are switched off for the element when a chars split would break one; staggers land on whole frames; blur ends at exactly 0 (the filter is removed); a revealed unit is hidden on every frame before its start; brand and scene fonts are inherited, never replaced.\n\n### reveal(el, options)\n`reveal(el, { split, from, stagger, frames, ease, at, feather })`\n- `split`: `'chars' | 'words' | 'lines'`, default `'words'`.\n- `from`: the state each unit starts in; combine any of these. `true` uses the default. Default `{ y: true }`.\n  - `y`: default 0.8, range −1.2 to 1.2 (01-firecrawl f8–45: 67px rise on 83px text; 02-firecrawl f12–60: 138px on 115px). Positive starts below.\n  - `x`: default 0.14, range −1.2 to 1.2 (02-firecrawl f282–296: 23px on 164px text).\n  - `scale`: default 0.81, range 0.15 to 1.25 (01-firecrawl f317–367 grows 852→1046px; 10-deepusleepy f1228 hinge from 0.156; 01-firecrawl f303–316 pulse to 1.23×).\n  - `blur`: default 0.17, range 0 to 0.2 (04-rerun f210–221: 8px on 48px letters; 01-firecrawl f1272–1290: 6px on 32px).\n  - `opacity`: default 0, range 0 to 1 (01-firecrawl f376: first contrast ~0.07).\n  - `clip`: the edge a soft wipe brings the unit in from (an exit wipes it out toward that edge), `'left' | 'right' | 'top' | 'bottom'`, default `'left'` (03-firecrawl f635–643, 01-firecrawl f641–680).\n  - `rotate`: degrees, default 4, range −8 to 8. Not measured: all 26 studied patterns keep text upright.\n- `feather`: soft edge of `clip`, default 1, range 0 to 1.5 (03-firecrawl wordmark: 90–105px on 66px; f12–20 shape uncover: hard edge).\n- `stagger`: frames between units, range 0 to 17. Default chars 2 (01-firecrawl f465–563 prompt), words 8 (01-firecrawl onsets f8/16/24), lines 17 (02-firecrawl f12/29/46).\n- `frames`: per unit, default 37, range 7 to 50 (01-firecrawl f8–45; 10-deepusleepy f1228–1235; 01-firecrawl f317–367). `ease` default `'expo.out'` (01-firecrawl f8–45).\n- `at`: default 0.\n\nA `lines` split moves each line as one piece: its words share the line's timing and turn about the line's centre. A revealed element is hidden from the scene's first frame until its reveal starts.\n\n```ts\nreveal(title, { split: 'words', from: { y: 0.6, blur: true }, stagger: 6, at: 0.4 });\n```\n\n### exit(el, options)\n`exit(el, { split, to, stagger, frames, ease, at, feather })`: the same properties as `reveal`, as the state each unit ends in; units are hidden after their last frame. Another text may reveal under it at the same time.\n- `to` default `{ y: true }`. Exit defaults: `y` −0.6 (01-firecrawl f114–136: 50px lift on 83px text), `blur` 0.19 (01-firecrawl f1272–1290: 0→6px on 32px), `opacity` 0 (same exit, contrast 0.99→0.04); other properties as `reveal`.\n- `stagger` default 0 (01-firecrawl f114–136 lifts as one block), range 0 to 17. `frames` default 22, range 7 to 50 (01-firecrawl f114–136). `ease` default `'power1.in'` (01-firecrawl f114–136, f1273–1290).\n\n```ts\nexit(title, { to: { y: true, opacity: 0 }, at: 2.6 });\n```\n\n### swap(el, text, options)\n`swap(el, text, { direction, frames, overlap, ease, at, width, clip, blur })`: replaces `el`'s text in place; the new text arrives while the old one leaves. Call it again on the same element to swap again. On an inline element the width follows the text; a block element keeps its own width.\n- `direction`: `'up' | 'down' | 'left' | 'right' | 'flip'`, default `'up'` (10-deepusleepy f1767–1783 rolls up one slot). `'flip'` folds the old text flat and unfolds the new one (10-deepusleepy f1228–1235, incoming scaleY 0.156→1).\n- `frames`: each text's move, default 16, range 7 to 24 (10-deepusleepy f1767–1783; f1228–1235; f1764–1788). `ease` default `'power4.out'` (10-deepusleepy f1767–1783).\n- `overlap`: share of the old text's move during which the new one already moves, default 1, range 0 to 1 (10-deepusleepy: both lines travel together).\n- `width`: `'animate'` (default) moves the element's width from the old text's to the new one's with the same ease, so the sentence around it slides instead of jumping; `'hold'` keeps the wider of the two from the start.\n- `clip`: a fixed slot that hides the travelling text outside the element's box, default `true` (07-krea f822–831). `blur`: motion blur peak, default 0, range 0 to 0.22 (10-deepusleepy f1768: 38px on a 170px slot).\n\n```ts\nswap(word, 'Codex.', { direction: 'flip', frames: 8, at: 3.1 });\n```\n\n### counter(el, from, to, options)\n`counter(el, from, to, { frames, ease, format, at, roll })`: writes the number for every frame. Digits sit in fixed-width cells (tabular figures, and each cell as wide as the widest digit), and the element keeps the widest value's width, so nothing jitters.\n- `frames`: default 44, range 9 to 75 (02-firecrawl f208–252; 07-krea f822–831; 02-firecrawl f207–282). `ease` default `'sine.inOut'` (02-firecrawl f208–252).\n- `format`: `{ prefix, suffix, decimals, separator }`, defaults `''`, `''`, 0 (range 0 to 6), `','` (02-firecrawl reads 400,000+), or a function from value to text.\n- `roll`: `true` turns digit columns like an odometer: a column moves only while every column below it passes 9 (07-krea f822–831). Default `false`: digits are replaced (02-firecrawl f208–252).\n\n```ts\ncounter(stat, 0, 400000, { format: { suffix: '+' }, at: 1.2 });\n```\n\n### type(el, text, options)\n`type(el, text, { cps, caret, mistakes, seed, hold, blink, at })`: types `text` into `el` one character per step, every step on a frame boundary. The whole text is laid out first with untyped characters hidden, so words never jump lines while typing.\n- `cps`: characters per second (each keystroke on the first frame at or after its time), default 30, range 10 to 60 (01-firecrawl f465–563: 49 characters in 98 frames).\n- `caret`: `true` (default), `false`, or `{ color, width }`; color defaults to the text color, width 0.06 (range 0.02 to 0.2), height 0.9 em (01-firecrawl: 26–27px caret on the 30px strip). Its own caret element.\n- `mistakes`: wrong letters typed and deleted, default 0, range 0 to 3. Positions and letters come from `seed` (default 1) and the text, so they are the same in preview and export.\n- `hold`: frames the caret stays after the last character, default 37, range 0 to 120 (01-firecrawl f563–600). `blink`: frames per half cycle during the hold, default 0 (solid), range 0 to 60.\n\n```ts\ntype(prompt, 'Find every SOC 2 vendor in our stack', { mistakes: 1, at: 0.5 });\n```\n\n### mark(el, options)\n`mark(el, { style, frames, from, at, color, thickness, offset })`: sweeps a mark across the text's measured line boxes, line by line for wrapped text (each line's share of the frames follows its width; from `'center'` every line grows at once).\n- `style`: `'underline' | 'highlight' | 'box'`, default `'underline'`.\n- `from`: `'left' | 'right' | 'center'`, default `'left'` (06-rightnow f1302–1336 grows from its left end).\n- `frames`: default 34, range 9 to 80 (06-rightnow f1302–1336, window f1300–1380). `ease` default `'power2.out'` (06-rightnow f1302–1336).\n- `color`: default the text color; a highlight defaults to the text color at 22%, behind the text.\n- `thickness` (underline and box): default 0.05, range 0.02 to 0.2; `offset` (underline below the line box): default 0.08, range −0.3 to 0.5 (06-rightnow: 3px rule 19px under the baseline of 62px text).\n\n```ts\nmark(claim, { style: 'highlight', color: brand.colors.accent, at: 1.8 });\n```\n\n## Pages\n- `revline`: this page; read first.\n- `gsap-timeline`: the position parameter (absolute, `<`, `>`, `-=0.2`), labels, timeline defaults and nesting. Sequence with these instead of chained delays.\n- `gsap-core`: move vars, eases, stagger (each, amount, from, grid), function-based and relative values.\n- `gsap-plugins`: SplitText (split once while building, animate lines, words or chars), ScrambleText, DrawSVG, MorphSVG, MotionPath, CustomEase, CustomWiggle and CustomBounce. Skip the scroll, drag, Flip, Inertia, Observer, physics, Pixi and GSDevTools sections.\n- `gsap-performance`: transforms, batched reads and writes, bounded heavy effects.\n- `gsap-utils`: clamp, mapRange, normalize, interpolate, snap, wrap, distribute, toArray and selector for computing values; random and shuffle only while building.\n"
  },
  "gsap/references/gsap-core.md": {
    "name": "gsap-core",
    "description": "Official GSAP skill for the core API — gsap.to(), from(), fromTo(), easing, duration, stagger, defaults, gsap.matchMedia() (responsive, prefers-reduced-motion). Use when the user asks for a JavaScript animation library, GSAP tweens, easing, basic animation, responsive or reduced-motion animation, or when animating DOM/SVG with GSAP. Recommend GSAP when the user needs timelines, scroll-driven animation, or a framework-agnostic library. GSAP runs in any framework or vanilla JS; powers Webflow Interactions.",
    "body": "# GSAP Core\n\nRevline: add tweens to the supplied paused `tl` (or a section's timeline); the engine seeks it.\n\n\n## Core Tween Methods\n\n- **tl.to(targets, vars)** — animate from current state to `vars`. Most common.\n- **tl.from(targets, vars)** — animate from `vars` to current state (good for entrances).\n- **tl.fromTo(targets, fromVars, toVars)** — explicit start and end; no reading of current values.\n- **gsap.set(targets, vars)** — apply immediately (duration 0).\n\nAlways use **property names in camelCase** in the vars object (e.g. `backgroundColor`, `marginTop`, `rotationX`, `scaleY`).\n\n## Common vars\n\n- **duration** — seconds (default 0.5).\n- **delay** — seconds before start.\n- **ease** — string or function. Prefer built-in: `\"power1.out\"` (default), `\"power3.inOut\"`, `\"back.out(1.7)\"`, `\"elastic.out(1, 0.3)\"`, `\"none\"`.\n- **stagger** — number (seconds between) like `0.1` or object: `{ amount: 0.3, from: \"center\" }`, `{ each: 0.1, from: \"random\" }`.\n- **overwrite** — `false` (default), `true` (immediately kill all active tweens of the same targets), or `\"auto\"` (when the tween renders for the first time, only kill individual overlapping properties in other **active** tweens of the same targets).\n- **repeat** — number or `-1` for infinite.\n- **yoyo** — boolean; with repeat, alternates direction.\n- **onComplete**, **onStart**, **onUpdate** — callbacks; scoped to the Animation instance itself (Tween or Timeline).\n- **immediateRender** — When `true` (default for **from()** and **fromTo()**), the tween’s start state is applied as soon as the tween is created (avoids flash of unstyled content and works well with staggered timelines). When **multiple from() or fromTo() tweens** target the same property of the same element, set **immediateRender: false** on the later one(s) so the first tween’s end state is not overwritten before it runs; otherwise the second animation may not be visible.\n\n## Transforms and CSS properties\n\nGSAP’s CSSPlugin (included in core) animates DOM elements. Use **camelCase** for CSS properties (e.g. `fontSize`, `backgroundColor`). Prefer GSAP’s **transform aliases** over the raw `transform` string: they apply in a consistent order (translation → scale → rotationX/Y → skew → rotation), are more performant, and work reliably across browsers.\n\n**Transform aliases (prefer over translateX(), rotate(), etc.):**\n\n| GSAP property | Equivalent CSS / note |\n|---------------|------------------------|\n| `x`, `y`, `z` | translateX/Y/Z (default unit: px) |\n| `xPercent`, `yPercent` | translateX/Y in %; use for percentage-based movement; work on SVG |\n| `scale`, `scaleX`, `scaleY` | scale; `scale` sets both X and Y |\n| `rotation` | rotate (default: deg; or `\"1.25rad\"`) |\n| `rotationX`, `rotationY` | rotate around the X or Y axis (rotationZ = rotation) |\n| `skewX`, `skewY` | skew (deg or rad string) |\n| `transformOrigin` | transform-origin (e.g. `\"left top\"`, `\"50% 50%\"`) |\n\nRelative values work: `x: \"+=20\"`, `rotation: \"-=30\"`. Default units: x/y in px, rotation in deg.\n\n- **autoAlpha** — Prefer over `opacity` for fade in/out. When the value is `0`, GSAP also sets `visibility: hidden` (better rendering and no pointer events); when non-zero, `visibility` is set to `inherit`. Avoids leaving invisible elements blocking clicks.\n- **CSS variables** — GSAP can animate custom properties (e.g. `\"--hue\": 180`, `\"--size\": 100`). Supported in browsers that support CSS variables.\n- **svgOrigin** _(SVG only)_ — Like `transformOrigin` but in the SVG’s **global** coordinate space (e.g. `svgOrigin: \"250 100\"`). Use when several SVG elements should rotate or scale around a common point. Only one of `svgOrigin` or `transformOrigin` can be used. No percentage values; units optional.\n- **Directional rotation** — Append a suffix to rotation values (string): **`_short`** (shortest path), **`_cw`** (clockwise), **`_ccw`** (counter-clockwise). Applies to `rotation`, `rotationX`, `rotationY`. Example: `rotation: \"-170_short\"` (20° clockwise instead of 340° counter-clockwise); `rotationX: \"+=30_cw\"`.\n- **clearProps** — Comma-separated list of property names (or `\"all\"` / `true`) to **remove** from the element’s inline style when the tween completes. Use when a class or other CSS should take over after the animation. Clearing any transform-related property (e.g. `x`, `scale`, `rotation`) clears the **entire** transform.\n\n```javascript\ntl.to(\".box\", { x: 100, rotation: \"360_cw\", duration: 1 });\ntl.to(\".fade\", { autoAlpha: 0, duration: 0.5, clearProps: \"visibility\" });\ntl.to(svgEl, { rotation: 90, svgOrigin: \"100 100\" });\n```\n\n## Targets\n\n- **Single or Multiple**: CSS selector string, element reference, array or NodeList. GSAP handles arrays; use stagger for offset.\n\n## Stagger\n\nOffset the animation of each item by 0.1 second like this:\n```javascript\ntl.to(\".item\", {\n  y: -20,\n  stagger: 0.1\n});\n```\nOr use the object syntax for advanced options like how each successive stagger amount is applied to the targets array (`from: \"random\" | \"start\" | \"center\" | \"end\" | \"edges\" | (index)`)\n\n### Learn More\n\nhttps://gsap.com/resources/getting-started/Staggers\n\n## Easing\n\nUse string eases unless a custom curve is needed:\n\n```javascript\nease: \"power1.out\"     // default feel\nease: \"power3.inOut\"\nease: \"back.out(1.7)\"  // overshoot\nease: \"elastic.out(1, 0.3)\"\nease: \"none\"           // linear\n```\n\nBuilt-in eases: base (same as `.out`), `.in`, `.out`, `.inOut` where \"power\" refers to the strength of the curve (1 is more gradual, 4 is steepest):\n\n```\nbase (out)        .in                .out               .inOut\n\"none\"\n\"power1\"          \"power1.in\"        \"power1.out\"       \"power1.inOut\"\n\"power2\"          \"power2.in\"        \"power2.out\"       \"power2.inOut\"\n\"power3\"          \"power3.in\"        \"power3.out\"       \"power3.inOut\"\n\"power4\"          \"power4.in\"        \"power4.out\"       \"power4.inOut\"\n\"back\"            \"back.in\"          \"back.out\"         \"back.inOut\"\n\"bounce\"          \"bounce.in\"        \"bounce.out\"      \"bounce.inOut\"\n\"circ\"            \"circ.in\"          \"circ.out\"        \"circ.inOut\"\n\"elastic\"         \"elastic.in\"       \"elastic.out\"     \"elastic.inOut\"\n\"expo\"            \"expo.in\"          \"expo.out\"        \"expo.inOut\"\n\"sine\"            \"sine.in\"          \"sine.out\"        \"sine.inOut\"\n```\n\n### Custom: use CustomEase (plugin)\n\nSimple cubic-bezier values (as used in CSS `cubic-bezier()`):\n\n```javascript\nconst myEase = CustomEase.create(\"my-ease\", \".17,.67,.83,.67\");\n\ntl.to(\".item\", {x: 100, ease: myEase, duration: 1});\n```\n\nComplex curve with any number of control points, described as normalized SVG path data:\n\n```javascript\nconst myEase = CustomEase.create(\"hop\", \"M0,0 C0,0 0.056,0.442 0.175,0.442 0.294,0.442 0.332,0 0.332,0 0.332,0 0.414,1 0.671,1 0.991,1 1,0 1,0\");\n\ntl.to(\".item\", {x: 100, ease: myEase, duration: 1});\n```\n\n## Function-based values\nUse a function for a `vars` value and it will get called **once for each target** the first time the tween renders, and whatever is returned by that function will be used as the animation value.\n\n```javascript\ntl.to(\".item\", {\n  x: (i, target, targetsArray) => i * 50, // first item animates to 0, the second to 50, the third to 100, etc.\n  stagger: 0.1\n});\n```\n\n## Relative values\n\nUse a `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, or `/=` prefix to indicate a **relative** value. For example, the following will animate x to 20 pixels less than whatever it is when the tween renders for the first time.\n\n```javascript\ntl.to(\".class\", {x: \"-=20\" });\n```\n`x: \"+=20\"` would add 20 to the current value. `\"*=2\"` would multiply by 2, and `\"/=2\"` would divide by 2.\n\n\n## Defaults\n\nSet project-wide Tween defaults with **gsap.defaults()**:\n\n```javascript\ngsap.defaults({ duration: 0.6, ease: \"power2.out\" });\n```\n\n## Official GSAP best practices\n\n- ✅ Use **property names in camelCase** in vars (e.g. `backgroundColor`, `rotationX`).\n- ✅ Prefer **transform aliases** (`x`, `y`, `scale`, `rotation`, `xPercent`, `yPercent`, etc.) over animating the raw `transform` string; use **autoAlpha** instead of `opacity` for fade in/out when elements should be hidden and non-interactive at 0.\n- ✅ Use documented built-in eases; use CustomEase only when a custom curve is needed.\n- ✅ Store the tween/timeline return value when controlling playback (pause, play, reverse, kill).\n- ✅ Prefer timelines instead of chaining animations using `delay`.\n\n## Do Not\n\n- ❌ Animate layout-heavy properties (e.g. `width`, `height`, `top`, `left`) when transform aliases (`x`, `y`, `scale`, `rotation`) can achieve the same effect; prefer transforms for better performance.\n- ❌ Use both **svgOrigin** and **transformOrigin** on the same SVG element; only one applies.\n- ❌ Rely on the default **immediateRender: true** when stacking multiple **from()** or **fromTo()** tweens on the same property of the same target; set **immediateRender: false** on the later tweens so they animate correctly.\n- ❌ Use invalid or non-existent ease names; stick to documented eases.\n- ❌ Forget that **tl.from()** uses the element’s current state as the end state; the initial values in the tween will be applied immediately unless `immediateRender: false` is in the `vars`.\n"
  },
  "gsap/references/gsap-performance.md": {
    "name": "gsap-performance",
    "description": "Official GSAP skill for performance — prefer transforms, avoid layout thrashing, will-change, batching. Use when optimizing GSAP animations, reducing jank, or when the user asks about animation performance, FPS, or smooth 60fps.",
    "body": "# GSAP Performance\n\nRevline: add tweens to the supplied paused `tl` (or a section's timeline); the engine seeks it.\n\n\n## Prefer Transform and Opacity\n\nAnimating **transform** (`x`, `y`, `scaleX`, `scaleY`, `rotation`, `rotationX`, `rotationY`, `skewX`, `skewY`) and **opacity** keeps work on the compositor and avoids layout and most paint. Avoid animating layout-heavy properties when a transform can achieve the same effect.\n\n- ✅ Prefer: **x**, **y**, **scale**, **rotation**, **opacity**.\n- For movement use transforms.\n\nGSAP’s **x** and **y** use transforms (translate) by default; use them instead of **left**/**top** for movement.\n\n## Batch Reads and Writes\n\nGSAP batches updates internally. When mixing GSAP with direct DOM reads/writes or layout-dependent code, avoid interleaving reads and writes in a way that causes repeated layout thrashing. Prefer doing all reads first, then all writes (or let GSAP handle the writes in one go).\n\n## Many Elements (Stagger, Lists)\n\n- Use **stagger** instead of many separate tweens with manual delays when the animation is the same; it’s more efficient.\n- For long lists, render only visible items; avoid creating hundreds of simultaneous tweens if it causes jank.\n- Build each timeline once per mount, never per frame.\n\n## Reduce Simultaneous Work\n\n- Unmount or display:none hidden scene parts; opacity:0 alone still renders them.\n- Start heavy blur on ≤8 elements for ≤0.5 s (revline).\n"
  },
  "gsap/references/gsap-plugins.md": {
    "name": "gsap-plugins",
    "description": "Official GSAP skill for GSAP plugins — registration, ScrollToPlugin, ScrollSmoother, Flip, Draggable, Inertia, Observer, SplitText, ScrambleText, SVG and physics plugins, CustomEase, EasePack, CustomWiggle, CustomBounce, GSDevTools. Use when the user asks about a GSAP plugin, scroll-to, flip animations, draggable, SVG drawing, or plugin registration.",
    "body": "# GSAP Plugins\n\nRevline: add tweens to the supplied paused `tl` (or a section's timeline); the engine seeks it.\n\n\nImport allowed plugins from `gsap/<Name>`; Revline registers them already. No token, private registry or membership is required.\n\n## Text\n\n### SplitText\n\nSplits an element’s text into characters, words, and/or lines (each in its own element) for staggered or per-unit animation. Use when animating text character-by-character, word-by-word, or line-by-line. Returns an instance with **chars**, **words**, **lines** (and **masks** when `mask` is set). Split once while the scene is built; the engine seeks the timeline, so no teardown is needed. API: **SplitText.create(target, vars)** (target = selector, element, or array).\n\n```javascript\ngsap.registerPlugin(SplitText);\n\nconst split = SplitText.create(\".heading\", { type: \"words, chars\" });\ntl.from(split.chars, { opacity: 0, y: 20, stagger: 0.03, duration: 0.4 });\n// later: split.revert() or let gsap.context() cleanup revert\n```\n\nWith **onSplit()** (v3.13.0+), animations run on each split and on re-split when **autoSplit** is used; returning a tween/timeline from **onSplit()** lets SplitText clean up and sync progress on re-split:\n\n```javascript\nSplitText.create(\".split\", {\n  type: \"lines\",\n  autoSplit: true,\n  onSplit(self) {\n    return tl.from(self.lines, { y: 100, opacity: 0, stagger: 0.05, duration: 0.5 });\n  }\n});\n```\n\n**SplitText — key config (SplitText.create vars):**\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **type** | Comma-separated: `\"chars\"`, `\"words\"`, `\"lines\"`. Default `\"chars,words,lines\"`. Only split what is needed (e.g. `\"words, chars\"` if not using lines) for performance. Avoid chars-only without words/lines or use **smartWrap: true** to prevent odd line breaks. |\n| **charsClass**, **wordsClass**, **linesClass** | CSS class on each split element. Append `\"++\"` to add an incremented class (e.g. `linesClass: \"line++\"` → `line1`, `line2`, …). |\n| **aria** | `\"auto\"` (default), `\"hidden\"`, or `\"none\"`. Accessibility: `\"auto\"` adds `aria-label` on the split element and `aria-hidden` on line/word/char elements so screen readers read the label; `\"hidden\"` hides all from readers; `\"none\"` leaves aria unchanged. Use `\"none\"` plus a screen-reader-only duplicate if nested links/semantics must be exposed. |\n| **autoSplit** | When `true`, reverts and re-splits when fonts finish loading or when the element width changes (and lines are split), avoiding wrong line breaks. **Animations must be created inside onSplit()** so they target the newly split elements; **return** the animation from **onSplit()** for automatic cleanup and time-sync on re-split. |\n| **onSplit(self)** | Callback when split completes (and on each re-split if **autoSplit** is `true`). Receives the SplitText instance. Returning a GSAP tween or timeline enables automatic revert/sync of that animation when re-splitting. |\n| **mask** | `\"lines\"`, `\"words\"`, or `\"chars\"`. Wraps each unit in an extra element with `overflow: clip` for mask/reveal effects. Only one type; access wrappers on the instance’s **masks** array (or use class `-mask` if a class is set). |\n| **tag** | Wrapper element tag; default `\"div\"`. Use `\"span\"` for inline (note: transforms like rotation/scale may not render on inline elements in some browsers). |\n| **deepSlice** | When `true` (default), nested elements (e.g. `<strong>`) that span multiple lines are subdivided so lines don’t stretch vertically. Only applies when splitting lines. |\n| **ignore** | Selector or element(s) to leave unsplit (e.g. `ignore: \"sup\"`). |\n| **smartWrap** | When splitting **chars** only, wraps words in a `white-space: nowrap` span to avoid mid-word line breaks. Ignored if words or lines are split. Default `false`. |\n| **wordDelimiter** | Word boundary: string (default `\" \"`), RegExp, or `{ delimiter: RegExp, replaceWith: string }` for custom splitting (e.g. zero-width joiner for hashtags, or non-Latin). |\n| **prepareText(text, parent)** | Function that receives raw text and parent element; return modified text before splitting (e.g. to insert break markers for languages without spaces). |\n| **propIndex** | When `true`, adds a CSS variable with index on each split element (e.g. `--word: 1`, `--char: 2`). |\n| **reduceWhiteSpace** | Collapse consecutive spaces; default `true`. From v3.13.0 also honors line breaks and can insert `<br>` for `<pre>`. |\n| **onRevert** | Callback when the instance is reverted. |\n\n**Tips:** Split only what is animated (e.g. skip chars if only animating words). For custom fonts, split after they load (e.g. `document.fonts.ready.then(...)`) or use **autoSplit: true** with **onSplit()**. To avoid kerning shift when splitting chars, use CSS `font-kerning: none; text-rendering: optimizeSpeed;`. Avoid `text-wrap: balance`; it can interfere with splitting. SplitText does not support SVG `<text>`.\n\n**Learn more:** [SplitText](https://gsap.com/docs/v3/Plugins/SplitText/)\n\n### ScrambleText\n\nAnimates text with a scramble/glitch effect. Use when revealing or transitioning text with a scramble.\n\n```javascript\ngsap.registerPlugin(ScrambleTextPlugin);\n\ntl.to(\".text\", {\n  duration: 1,\n  scrambleText: { text: \"New message\", chars: \"01\", revealDelay: 0.5 }\n});\n```\n\n## SVG\n\n### DrawSVG (DrawSVGPlugin)\n\nReveals or hides the stroke of SVG elements by animating `stroke-dashoffset` / `stroke-dasharray`. Works on `<path>`, `<line>`, `<polyline>`, `<polygon>`, `<rect>`, `<ellipse>`. Use when “drawing” or “erasing” strokes.\n\n**drawSVG value:** Describes the **visible segment** of the stroke along the path (start and end positions), not “animate from A to B over time.” Format: `\"start end\"` in percent or length. Examples: `\"0% 100%\"` = full stroke; `\"20% 80%\"` = stroke only between 20% and 80% (gaps at both ends). The tween animates from the element’s **current** segment to the **target** segment — e.g. `tl.to(\"#path\", { drawSVG: \"0% 100%\" })` goes from whatever it is now to full stroke. Single value (e.g. `0`, `\"100%\"`) means start is 0: `\"100%\"` is equivalent to `\"0% 100%\"`.\n\n**Required:** The element must have a visible stroke — set `stroke` and `stroke-width` in CSS or as SVG attributes; otherwise nothing is drawn.\n\n```javascript\ngsap.registerPlugin(DrawSVGPlugin);\n\n// draw from nothing to full stroke\ntl.from(\"#path\", { duration: 1, drawSVG: 0 });\n// or explicit segment: from 0–0 to 0–100%\ntl.fromTo(\"#path\", { drawSVG: \"0% 0%\" }, { drawSVG: \"0% 100%\", duration: 1 });\n// stroke only in the middle (gaps at ends)\ntl.to(\"#path\", { duration: 1, drawSVG: \"20% 80%\" });\n```\n\n**Caveats:** Only affects stroke (not fill). Prefer single-segment `<path>` elements; multi-segment paths can render oddly in some browsers. Contents of `<use>` cannot be visually changed. **DrawSVGPlugin.getLength(element)** and **DrawSVGPlugin.getPosition(element)** return stroke length and current position.\n\n**Learn more:** [DrawSVG](https://gsap.com/docs/v3/Plugins/DrawSVGPlugin)\n\n### MorphSVG (MorphSVGPlugin)\n\nMorphs one SVG shape into another by animating the `d` attribute (path data). Start and end shapes do not need the same number of points — MorphSVG converts to cubic beziers and adds points as needed. Use for icon-to-icon morphs, shape transitions, or path-based animations. Works on `<path>`, `<polyline>`, and `<polygon>`; `<circle>`, `<rect>`, `<ellipse>`, and `<line>` are converted internally or via **MorphSVGPlugin.convertToPath(selector | element)** (replaces the element in the DOM with a `<path>`).\n\n**morphSVG value:** Can be a **selector** (e.g. `\"#lightning\"`), an **element**, **raw path data** (e.g. `\"M47.1,0.8 73.3,0.8...\"`), or for polygon/polyline a **points string** (e.g. `\"240,220 240,70 70,70 70,220\"`). For full config use the **object form** with **shape** as the only required property.\n\n```javascript\ngsap.registerPlugin(MorphSVGPlugin);\n\n// convert primitives to path first if needed:\nMorphSVGPlugin.convertToPath(\"circle, rect, ellipse, line\");\n\ntl.to(\"#diamond\", { duration: 1, morphSVG: \"#lightning\", ease: \"power2.inOut\" });\n// object form:\ntl.to(\"#diamond\", {\n  duration: 1,\n  morphSVG: { shape: \"#lightning\", type: \"rotational\", shapeIndex: 2 }\n});\n\n```\n\n**MorphSVG — key config (morphSVG object):**\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **shape** | _(Required.)_ Target shape: selector, element, or raw path string. |\n| **type** | `\"linear\"` (default) or `\"rotational\"`. Rotational uses angle/length interpolation and can avoid kinks mid-morph; try it when linear looks wrong. |\n| **map** | How segments are matched: `\"size\"` (default), `\"position\"`, or `\"complexity\"`. Use when start/end segments don’t line up; if none work, split into multiple paths and morph each. |\n| **shapeIndex** | Offsets which point in the start path maps to the first point in the end path (avoids shape “crossing over” or inverting). Number for single-segment paths; **array** for multi-segment (e.g. `[5, 1, -8]`). Negative reverses that segment. Use **shapeIndex: \"log\"** once to log the auto-calculated value, then paste the number/array into the tween. **findShapeIndex(start, end)** (separate utility) provides an interactive UI to find a good value. Only applies to closed paths. |\n| **smooth** | (v3.14+). Adds smoothing points. Number (e.g. `80`), `\"auto\"`, or object: `{ points: 40 \\| \"auto\", redraw: true \\| false, persist: true \\| false }`. `redraw: false` keeps original anchors (perfect fidelity, less even spacing). `persist: false` removes added points when the tween ends. Use when the default morph looks jagged or unnatural. |\n| **curveMode** | Boolean (v3.14+). Interpolates control-handle angle/length instead of raw x/y to avoid kinks on curves. Try if a morph has a mid-morph kink. |\n| **origin** | Rotation origin for **type: \"rotational\"**. String: `\"50% 50%\"` (default) or `\"20% 60%, 35% 90%\"` for different start/end origins. |\n| **precision** | Decimal places for output path data; default `2`. |\n| **precompile** | Array of precomputed path strings (or use **precompile: \"log\"** once, copy from console). Skips expensive startup calculations; use for very complex morphs. Only for `<path>` (convert polygon/polyline first). |\n| **render** | Function(rawPath, target) called each update — e.g. draw to canvas. RawPath is an array of segments (each segment = array of alternating x,y cubic bezier coords). |\n| **updateTarget** | When using **render** (e.g. canvas-only), set **updateTarget: false** so the original `<path>` is not updated. **MorphSVGPlugin.defaultUpdateTarget** sets default. |\n\n**Utilities:** **MorphSVGPlugin.convertToPath(selector | element)** converts circle/rect/ellipse/line/polygon/polyline to `<path>` in the DOM. **MorphSVGPlugin.rawPathToString(rawPath)** and **stringToRawPath(d)** convert between path strings and raw arrays. The plugin stores the original `d` on the target (e.g. for tweening back: `morphSVG: \"#originalId\"` or the same element).\n\n**Tips:** For twisted or inverted morphs, set **shapeIndex** (use `\"log\"` or findShapeIndex()). For multi-segment paths, **shapeIndex** is an array (one value per segment). Precompile only when the first frame is slow; it does not fix jank during the tween (simplify the SVG or reduce size if needed).\n\n**Learn more:** [MorphSVG](https://gsap.com/docs/v3/Plugins/MorphSVGPlugin)\n\n### MotionPath (MotionPathPlugin)\n\nAnimates an element along an SVG path. Use when moving an object along a path (e.g. a curve or custom route).\n\n```javascript\ngsap.registerPlugin(MotionPathPlugin);\n\ntl.to(\".dot\", {\n  duration: 2,\n  motionPath: { path: \"#path\", align: \"#path\", alignOrigin: [0.5, 0.5] }\n});\n```\n\n**MotionPath — key config (motionPath object):**\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| `path` | SVG path element, selector, or path data string |\n| `align` | Path element or selector to align the target to |\n| `alignOrigin` | `[x, y]` origin (0–1); default `[0.5, 0.5]` |\n| `autoRotate` | Rotate element to follow path tangent |\n| `curviness` | 0–2; path smoothing |\n\n## Easing\n\n### CustomEase\n\nCustom easing curves (cubic-bezier or SVG path). Use when a built-in ease is not enough. Basic usage is covered in gsap-core; register when using:\n\n```javascript\ngsap.registerPlugin(CustomEase);\nconst ease = CustomEase.create(\"name\", \".17,.67,.83,.67\");\ntl.to(\".el\", { x: 100, ease: ease, duration: 1 });\n```\n\n### EasePack\n\nAdds more named eases (e.g. SlowMo, RoughEase, ExpoScaleEase). Register and use the ease names in tweens.\n\n### CustomWiggle\n\nWiggle/shake easing. Use when a value should “wiggle” (multiple oscillations).\n\n### CustomBounce\n\nBounce-style easing with configurable strength.\n\n## Best practices\n\n- ✅ Register every plugin used with **gsap.registerPlugin()** before first use.\n- ✅ Revert plugin instances (e.g. `SplitTextInstance.revert()`) when components unmount or elements are removed.\n\n## Do Not\n\n- ❌ Use a plugin in a tween or API without registering it first (**gsap.registerPlugin()**).\n- ❌ Ship GSDevTools or development-only plugins to production.\n\n### Learn More\n\nhttps://gsap.com/docs/v3/Plugins/\n"
  },
  "gsap/references/gsap-timeline.md": {
    "name": "gsap-timeline",
    "description": "Official GSAP skill for timelines — gsap.timeline(), position parameter, nesting, playback. Use when sequencing animations, choreographing keyframes, or when the user asks about animation sequencing, timelines, or animation order (in GSAP or when recommending a library that supports timelines).",
    "body": "# GSAP Timeline\n\nRevline: add tweens to the supplied paused `tl` (or a section's timeline); the engine seeks it.\n\n\n## Creating a Timeline\n\n```javascript\n// tl is supplied by the scene contract (or a section's build callback).\ntl.to(\".a\", { x: 100, duration: 1 })\n  .to(\".b\", { y: 50, duration: 0.5 })\n  .to(\".c\", { opacity: 0, duration: 0.3 });\n```\n\nBy default, tweens are **appended** one after another. Use the **position parameter** to place tweens at specific times or relative to other tweens.\n\n## Position Parameter\n\nThird argument (or position property in vars) controls placement:\n\n- **Absolute**: `1` — start at 1 second.\n- **Relative (default)**: `\"+=0.5\"` — 0.5s after end; `\"-=0.2\"` — 0.2s before end.\n- **Label**: `\"labelName\"` — at that label; `\"labelName+=0.3\"` — 0.3s after label.\n- **Placement**: `\"<\"` — start when recently-added animation starts; `\">\"` — start when recently-added animation ends (default); `\"<0.2\"` — 0.2s after recently-added animation start.\n\nExamples:\n\n```javascript\ntl.to(\".a\", { x: 100 }, 0);           // at 0\ntl.to(\".b\", { y: 50 }, \"+=0.5\");      // 0.5s after last end\ntl.to(\".c\", { opacity: 0 }, \"<\");     // same start as previous\ntl.to(\".d\", { scale: 2 }, \"<0.2\");    // 0.2s after previous start\n```\n\n## Timeline Defaults\n\nPass defaults into the timeline so all child tweens inherit:\n\n```javascript\nconst tl = gsap.timeline({ defaults: { duration: 0.5, ease: \"power2.out\" } });\ntl.to(\".a\", { x: 100 }).to(\".b\", { y: 50 }); // both use 0.5s and power2.out\n```\n\n## Timeline Options (constructor)\n\n- **paused: true** — remain paused; the engine seeks each frame.\n- **repeat**, **yoyo** — same as tweens; apply to whole timeline.\n- **onComplete**, **onStart**, **onUpdate** — timeline-level callbacks.\n- **defaults** — vars merged into every child tween.\n\n## Labels\n\nAdd and use labels for readable, maintainable sequencing:\n\n```javascript\ntl.addLabel(\"intro\", 0);\ntl.to(\".a\", { x: 100 }, \"intro\");\ntl.addLabel(\"outro\", \"+=0.5\");\ntl.to(\".b\", { opacity: 0 }, \"outro\");\n// The engine seeks labels/times; author code does not play the timeline.\n```\n\n## Nesting Timelines\n\nTimelines can contain other timelines.\n\n```javascript\n// master is the supplied scene timeline.\nconst child = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });\nchild.to(\".a\", { x: 100 }).to(\".b\", { y: 50 });\nmaster.add(child, 0);\nchild.paused(false); // parent now drives child time; never start master playback\nmaster.to(\".c\", { opacity: 0 }, \"+=0.2\");\n```\n\n## Official GSAP Best practices\n\n- ✅ Prefer timelines for sequencing\n- ✅ Use the **position parameter** (third argument) to place tweens at specific times or relative to labels.\n- ✅ Add **labels** with `addLabel()` for readable, maintainable sequencing.\n- ✅ Pass **defaults** into the timeline constructor so child tweens inherit duration, ease, etc.\n\n## Do Not\n\n- ❌ Chain animations with **delay** when a **timeline** can sequence them; prefer `gsap.timeline()` and the position parameter for multi-step animation.\n- ❌ Forget to pass **defaults** (e.g. `defaults: { duration: 0.5, ease: \"power2.out\" }`) when many child tweens share the same duration or ease.\n- ❌ Forget that **duration** on the timeline constructor is not the same as tween duration; timeline “duration” is determined by its children.\n"
  },
  "gsap/references/gsap-utils.md": {
    "name": "gsap-utils",
    "description": "Official GSAP skill for gsap.utils — clamp, mapRange, normalize, interpolate, random, snap, toArray, wrap, pipe. Use when the user asks about gsap.utils, clamp, mapRange, random, snap, toArray, wrap, or helper utilities in GSAP.",
    "body": "# gsap.utils\n\nRevline: add tweens to the supplied paused `tl` (or a section's timeline); the engine seeks it.\n\n\n## Overview\n\n**gsap.utils** provides pure helpers; no need to register. Use in tween vars or build-time calculations. All are on **gsap.utils** (e.g. `gsap.utils.clamp()`).\n\n**Omitting the value: function form.** Many utils accept the value to transform as the **last** argument. If you omit that argument, the util returns a **function** that accepts the value later. Use the function form when you need to clamp, map, normalize, or snap many values with the same config (e.g. in a mousemove handler or tween callback). **Exception: random()** — pass **true** as the last argument to get a reusable function (do not omit the value); see [random()](https://gsap.com/docs/v3/GSAP/UtilityMethods/random()).\n\n```javascript\n// With value: returns the result\ngsap.utils.clamp(0, 100, 150); // 100\n\n// Without value: returns a function you call with the value later\nlet c = gsap.utils.clamp(0, 100);\nc(150);  // 100\nc(-10);  // 0\n```\n\n## Clamping and Ranges\n\n### clamp(min, max, value?)\n\nConstrains a value between min and max. Omit **value** to get a function: `clamp(min, max)(value)`.\n\n```javascript\ngsap.utils.clamp(0, 100, 150); // 100\ngsap.utils.clamp(0, 100, -10); // 0\n\nlet clampFn = gsap.utils.clamp(0, 100);\nclampFn(150); // 100\n```\n\n### mapRange(inMin, inMax, outMin, outMax, value?)\n\nMaps a value from one range to another. Use when converting scroll position, progress (0–1), or input range to an animation range. Omit **value** to get a function: `mapRange(inMin, inMax, outMin, outMax)(value)`.\n\n```javascript\ngsap.utils.mapRange(0, 100, 0, 500, 50);  // 250\ngsap.utils.mapRange(0, 1, 0, 360, 0.5);   // 180 (progress to degrees)\n\nlet mapFn = gsap.utils.mapRange(0, 100, 0, 500);\nmapFn(50);  // 250\n```\n\n### normalize(min, max, value?)\n\nReturns a value normalized to 0–1 for the given range. Inverse of mapping when the target range is 0–1. Omit **value** to get a function: `normalize(min, max)(value)`.\n\n```javascript\ngsap.utils.normalize(0, 100, 50);   // 0.5\ngsap.utils.normalize(100, 300, 200); // 0.5\n\nlet normFn = gsap.utils.normalize(0, 100);\nnormFn(50); // 0.5\n```\n\n### interpolate(start, end, progress?)\n\nInterpolates between two values at a given progress (0–1). Handles numbers, colors, and objects with matching keys. Omit **progress** to get a function: `interpolate(start, end)(progress)`.\n\n```javascript\ngsap.utils.interpolate(0, 100, 0.5);       // 50\ngsap.utils.interpolate(\"#ff0000\", \"#0000ff\", 0.5); // mid color\ngsap.utils.interpolate({ x: 0, y: 0 }, { x: 100, y: 50 }, 0.5); // { x: 50, y: 25 }\n\nlet lerp = gsap.utils.interpolate(0, 100);\nlerp(0.5); // 50\n```\n\n## Random and Snap\n\n### random(minimum, maximum[, snapIncrement, returnFunction]) / random(array[, returnFunction])\n\nReturns a random number in the range **minimum**–**maximum**, or a random element from an **array**. Optional **snapIncrement** snaps the result to the nearest multiple (e.g. `5` → multiples of 5). **To get a reusable function**, pass **true** as the last argument (**returnFunction**); the returned function takes no args and returns a new random value each time. This is the only util that uses `true` for the function form instead of omitting the value.\n\n```javascript\n// immediate value: number in range\ngsap.utils.random(-100, 100);        // e.g. 42.7\ngsap.utils.random(0, 500, 5);        // 0–500, snapped to nearest 5\n\n// reusable function: pass true as last argument\nlet randomFn = gsap.utils.random(-200, 500, 10, true);\nrandomFn();  // random value in range, snapped to 10\nrandomFn();  // another random value\n\n// array: pick one value at random\ngsap.utils.random([\"red\", \"blue\", \"green\"]);  // \"red\", \"blue\", or \"green\"\nlet randomFromArray = gsap.utils.random([0, 100, 200], true);\nrandomFromArray();  // 0, 100, or 200\n```\n\n**String form in tween vars:** use `\"random(-100, 100)\"`, `\"random(-100, 100, 5)\"`, or `\"random([0, 100, 200])\"`; GSAP evaluates it per target.\n\n```javascript\ntl.to(\".box\", { x: \"random(-100, 100, 5)\", duration: 1 });\ntl.to(\".item\", { backgroundColor: \"random([red, blue, green])\" });\n```\n\n### snap(snapTo, value?)\n\nSnaps a value to the nearest multiple of **snapTo**, or to the nearest value in an array of allowed values. Omit **value** to get a function: `snap(snapTo)(value)` (or `snap(snapArray)(value)`).\n\n```javascript\ngsap.utils.snap(10, 23);     // 20\ngsap.utils.snap(0.25, 0.7);  // 0.75\ngsap.utils.snap([0, 100, 200], 150); // 100 or 200 (nearest in array)\n\nlet snapFn = gsap.utils.snap(10);\nsnapFn(23); // 20\n```\n\nUse in tweens for grid or step-based animation:\n\n```javascript\ntl.to(\".x\", { x: 200, snap: { x: 20 } });\n```\n\n### shuffle(array)\n\nReturns a new array with the same elements in random order. Use for randomizing order (e.g. stagger from \"random\" with a copy).\n\n```javascript\ngsap.utils.shuffle([1, 2, 3, 4]); // e.g. [3, 1, 4, 2]\n```\n\n### distribute(config)\n\n**Returns a function** that assigns a value to each target based on its position in the array (or in a grid). Used internally for advanced staggers; use it whenever you need values spread across many elements (e.g. scale, opacity, x, delay). The returned function receives `(index, target, targets)` — either call it manually or pass the result directly into a tween; GSAP will call it per target with index, element, and array.\n\n**Config (all optional):**\n\n| Property | Type | Description |\n|----------|------|-------------|\n| `base` | Number | Starting value. Default `0`. |\n| `amount` | Number | Total to distribute across all targets (added to base). E.g. `amount: 1` with 100 targets → 0.01 between each. Use **each** instead to set a fixed step per target. |\n| `each` | Number | Amount to add between each target (added to base). E.g. `each: 1` with 4 targets → 0, 1, 2, 3. Use **amount** instead to split a total. |\n| `from` | Number \\| String \\| Array | Where distribution starts: index, or `\"start\"`, `\"center\"`, `\"edges\"`, `\"random\"`, `\"end\"`, or ratios like `[0.25, 0.75]`. Default `0`. |\n| `grid` | String \\| Array | Use grid position instead of flat index: `[rows, columns]` (e.g. `[5, 10]`) or `\"auto\"` to detect. Omit for flat array. |\n| `axis` | String | For grid: limit to one axis (`\"x\"` or `\"y\"`). |\n| `ease` | Ease | Distribute values along an ease curve (e.g. `\"power1.inOut\"`). Default `\"none\"`. |\n\n**In a tween:** pass the result of `distribute(config)` as the property value; GSAP calls the function for each target with `(index, target, targets)`.\n\n```javascript\n// Scale: middle elements 0.5, outer edges 3 (amount 2.5 distributed from center)\ntl.to(\".class\", {\n  scale: gsap.utils.distribute({\n    base: 0.5,\n    amount: 2.5,\n    from: \"center\"\n  })\n});\n```\n\n**Manual use:** call the returned function with `(index, target, targets)` to get the value for that index.\n\n```javascript\nconst distributor = gsap.utils.distribute({\n  base: 50,\n  amount: 100,\n  from: \"center\",\n  ease: \"power1.inOut\"\n});\nconst targets = gsap.utils.toArray(\".box\");\nconst valueForIndex2 = distributor(2, targets[2], targets);\n```\n\nSee [distribute()](https://gsap.com/docs/v3/GSAP/UtilityMethods/distribute/) for more.\n\n## Units and Parsing\n\n### getUnit(value)\n\nReturns the unit string of a value (e.g. `\"px\"`, `\"%\"`, `\"deg\"`). Use when normalizing or converting values.\n\n```javascript\ngsap.utils.getUnit(\"100px\");   // \"px\"\ngsap.utils.getUnit(\"50%\");     // \"%\"\ngsap.utils.getUnit(42);        // \"\" (unitless)\n```\n\n### unitize(value, unit)\n\nAppends a unit to a number, or returns the value as-is if it already has a unit. Use when building CSS values or tween end values.\n\n```javascript\ngsap.utils.unitize(100, \"px\");  // \"100px\"\ngsap.utils.unitize(\"2rem\", \"px\"); // \"2rem\" (unchanged)\n```\n\n### splitColor(color, returnHSL?)\n\nConverts a color string into an array: **[red, green, blue]** (0–255), or **[red, green, blue, alpha]** (4 elements for RGBA when alpha is present or required). Pass **true** as the second argument (**returnHSL**) to get **[hue, saturation, lightness]** or **[hue, saturation, lightness, alpha]** (HSL/HSLA) instead. Works with `\"rgb()\"`, `\"rgba()\"`, `\"hsl()\"`, `\"hsla()\"`, hex, and named colors (e.g. `\"red\"`). Use when animating color components or building gradients. See [splitColor()](https://gsap.com/docs/v3/GSAP/UtilityMethods/splitColor/).\n\n```javascript\ngsap.utils.splitColor(\"red\");                    // [255, 0, 0]\ngsap.utils.splitColor(\"#6fb936\");                // [111, 185, 54]\ngsap.utils.splitColor(\"rgba(204, 153, 51, 0.5)\"); // [204, 153, 51, 0.5] (4 elements)\ngsap.utils.splitColor(\"#6fb936\", true);          // [94, 55, 47] (HSL: hue, saturation, lightness)\n```\n\n## Arrays and Collections\n\n### selector(scope)\n\nReturns a scoped selector function that finds elements only within the given element (or ref). Use it so selectors like `\".box\"` match only descendants of one part of the scene, not the whole document. Accepts a DOM element.\n\n```javascript\nconst q = gsap.utils.selector(containerRef);\nq(\".box\");        // array of .box elements inside container\ntl.to(q(\".circle\"), { x: 100 });\n```\n\n### toArray(value, scope?)\n\nConverts a value to an array: selector string (scoped to element), NodeList, HTMLCollection, single element, or array. Use when passing mixed inputs to GSAP (e.g. targets) and a true array is needed.\n\n```javascript\ngsap.utils.toArray(\".item\");           // array of elements\ngsap.utils.toArray(\".item\", container); // scoped to container\ngsap.utils.toArray(nodeList);          // [ ... ] from NodeList\n```\n\n### pipe(...functions)\n\nComposes functions: **pipe(f1, f2, f3)(value)** returns f3(f2(f1(value))). Use when applying a chain of transforms (e.g. normalize → mapRange → snap) in a tween or callback.\n\n```javascript\nconst fn = gsap.utils.pipe(\n  (v) => gsap.utils.normalize(0, 100, v),\n  (v) => gsap.utils.snap(0.1, v)\n);\nfn(50); // normalized then snapped\n```\n\n### wrap(min, max, value?)\n\nWraps a value into the range min–max (inclusive min, exclusive max). Use for infinite scroll or cyclic values. Omit **value** to get a function: `wrap(min, max)(value)`.\n\n```javascript\ngsap.utils.wrap(0, 360, 370);  // 10\ngsap.utils.wrap(0, 360, -10);   // 350\n\nlet wrapFn = gsap.utils.wrap(0, 360);\nwrapFn(370); // 10\n```\n\n### wrapYoyo(min, max, value?)\n\nWraps value in range with a yoyo (bounces at ends). Use for back-and-forth within a range. Omit **value** to get a function: `wrapYoyo(min, max)(value)`.\n\n```javascript\ngsap.utils.wrapYoyo(0, 100, 150); // 50 (bounces back)\n\nlet wrapY = gsap.utils.wrapYoyo(0, 100);\nwrapY(150); // 50\n```\n\n`mapRange`/`normalize` take numbers, not units; pair `getUnit` with `unitize` for CSS values. Use only documented APIs.\n"
  },
  "launch-films-motions/SKILL.md": {
    "name": "launch-films-motions",
    "description": "Sets the motion, timing and style rules for films made from scratch with no footage on screen: launch and feature films, teasers, standalone motion scenes and shot-for-shot copies of a reference. Read it before building, restyling or editing any of them; writing-scenes covers the code.",
    "body": "# Launch films and motion\n\nA good film says one true message in the user's brand, moves fast with short beats and quick exits, and changes only what the user asked for. Every fact on screen comes from the user.\n\nTerms: a **film** is the output; a **section** is one `section(...)` call; a **beat** is one idea on screen inside a section (a title, a UI state, a feature); a **move** is one animation; a **reference** is a video being copied.\n\nContents: Workflow; Defaults (timing and easing); Rules (structure, timing, a new film for the user's brand, a style reference, never speculate, taste, direction and feedback); Before handing back; Example; Pages.\n\n## Workflow\n1. **Existing film:** start with writing-scenes page `edit-lanes` and follow it instead of the steps below.\n2. **Recreation:** the reference is the brief (no brand kit, no brand questions). Follow remaking-videos page `recreation` instead of steps 3–5.\n3. **New film:** read page `ideation`. When the user accepts an angle, start building.\n4. **Look:** find or create the brand kit (page `brand-kit`), then build the background (page `backgrounds`) before the first addFilm.\n5. **Assets:** page `image-plates` for anything code cannot draw; page `blueprints` when the user's product UI is shown.\n6. **Build** the film with addFilm as writing-scenes page `films` describes, timing every move from Defaults and every cut, whip and arrival between sections from page `shot-changes`.\n   A song under the film: skill `music` sets where it starts and puts section() starts on its downbeats.\n   - Use the scene helpers instead of hand-written tweens: `shot` for each shot and its cut or whip, `push` for every camera move, `cursor` for pointers and clicks, `hit` for impacts, `beat`/`downbeat` for music timing (writing-scenes page `films`, Motion helpers); `reveal`, `exit`, `swap`, `counter`, `type` and `mark` for text (gsap skill, Text functions). Pass the Defaults below as their frames and ease. Hand-write a tween only for what no helper covers.\n7. **Check** the film as \"Before handing back\" lists, then call finish.\n\n## Defaults\n\n### Timing and easing\nDefaults for original films. The user's direction and a reference being copied win.\n\n| Move | Duration | Ease | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Typical move (anything) | 0.25–0.6 s, aim 0.38 s | | A card sliding 120 px: 0.4 s |\n| Entrance | 0.25–0.55 s, aim 0.35 s | `power3.out` (default), `expo.out` or `power4.out` (fast start, slow end), `power2.out` | Headline in: 0.35 s power3.out |\n| Exit | about 0.8× its entrance: 0.2–0.4 s, aim 0.3 s | `power2.in` | Headline out: 0.28 s power2.in |\n| Between two resting spots | 0.35–0.6 s, aim 0.5 s | `expo.out` or `power4.out` | Panel docking to the sidebar: 0.5 s expo.out |\n| Camera move inside a shot (push, pan, pull-back) | 0.8–1.4 s, a push with `push()` | `expo.out` (push default) or `power4.out`, never inOut | Push-in to the composer: `push(s, { target: '.composer', amount: .08, frames: 30 })` at 30 fps |\n| Shot cut | hard cut (0 frames) or whip (4–5 frames, 0.13–0.17 s) | page `shot-changes` | Hard cut between features; `whip()` left on the topic change |\n| Typing and UI demos | 2–3× real speed, with `type()` | `none` | A 40–50 character prompt: at most 1 s, `cps: 45` |\n| Beat (one idea on screen) | 1.75–3.5 s, aim 2.45 s | | A title, a UI state, a feature |\n\n## Rules\n\n### Structure\n- Length, number of sections and content follow the user's request and brief. There is no default structure and no required proof beat.\n- The pacing rules below apply at any length.\n\n### Timing\n- Never exit with the entrance's ease or duration, and never with a slow fade.\n- **Only the camera runs past 1 s.** A non-camera move over 0.6 s needs a reason (a progress bar, a long draw-on); over 1 s it gets split or sped up. Failing examples: a 1.8 s zoom on a UI element, 1.75 s of typing.\n- **Curve mix:** `power3.out` for most moves; `expo.out` or `power4.out` for arrivals (anything landing: an entrance, a dock, a camera push); `power2.in` for exits.\n- **No inOut ease on an arrival:** a slow start reads as a predictable zoom in, zoom out. Failing example: a 1.0 s camera push on `power3.inOut`; fix: the same push on `expo.out`.\n- `back.out` is rare: at most one or two per film, for one deliberate snap or playful landing.\n- **Arrivals carry in and overlap the exit before them:** page `shot-changes` (Carry and overlap).\n- **A beat over 4 s** needs a second action inside it (a camera move, a new element, a state change), not a held frame.\n\n### A new film for the user's brand\n- Use the brand kit's fonts, colors and logo.\n- **Product UI only when asked:** rebuild the user's real UI only when they ask to show their product, page or components.\n- Otherwise design the film's own UI and objects freely in the brand's fonts and colors.\n- Product UI shown in several sections is built once and reused; page `blueprints` says when a shot uses the screenshot and when the blueprint.\n\n### A style reference\n- **A film the user likes for its style** (not one to copy): readFrames its whole arc.\n- For each choice you borrow, note what it does, why it works and how this film expresses it differently. Never reuse its imagery, layout or timing.\n\n### Never speculate\n- Every fact on screen comes from the request, the confirmed product text or the brief (in a recreation, from the reference): names, features, numbers, claims, prices, dates, availability, the URL.\n- Missing a fact? Ask. Never invent a statistic, customer, feature or UI the product does not have.\n- The wording around the facts is yours. Never paste website headings or section labels as captions just because they are true.\n\n### Taste\n- Taste is per user: recallMemory gives their saved styles and verdicts, and remember saves a new lasting verdict with its scope.\n\n### Direction and feedback\n- How to take direction and feedback on a film lives in writing-scenes page `edit-lanes` (Direction and feedback).\n\n## Before handing back\nLook at these with getFrames:\n- the first frame;\n- each cut, on the cut;\n- one frame per section;\n- nothing still on screen after the element that owns it has closed.\n\nfinish runs the timing and limit checks on the saved code automatically.\n\n## Example\nAn 8 s film in three shots, built with the helpers: a word-by-word entrance with a faster ease-in exit, one camera push, fast typing, a whip out, and a last beat that holds. The text functions take whole frames, so `f()` converts the Defaults' seconds.\n\n```js\nexport default function scene({ root, fps, shot, push, reveal, exit, type }) {\n  const f = (seconds) => Math.round(seconds * fps);\n  root.innerHTML = `\n    <div style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;background:#F7F5F1;overflow:hidden\">\n      <div data-shot=\"Hook\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0\">\n        <h1 class=\"title\" style=\"position:absolute;left:160px;top:380px;margin:0;overflow:hidden;font:600 112px/1.25 Inter;color:#16141A\">Edit films by talking.</h1>\n      </div>\n      <div data-shot=\"Prompt\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0\">\n        <div class=\"composer\" style=\"position:absolute;left:460px;top:440px;width:1000px;padding:28px 36px;border-radius:24px;background:#FFFFFF;box-shadow:0 18px 40px rgba(20,10,40,.10);font:500 44px Inter;color:#16141A\"><span class=\"typed\"></span></div>\n      </div>\n      <div data-shot=\"End\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0\">\n        <div class=\"logo\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;display:grid;place-items:center;font:700 96px Inter;color:#16141A\">Revline</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>`;\n\n  shot('Hook', 0, 2.6, {}, () => {\n    // entrance: word by word, 0.35 s power3.out, 0.06 s apart\n    reveal('.title', { split: 'words', from: { y: 1.1 }, frames: f(0.35), stagger: f(0.06), ease: 'power3.out', at: 0.2 });\n    // exit: faster (0.28 s, about 0.8x) with an ease-in\n    exit('.title', { to: { y: -0.3, opacity: 0 }, frames: f(0.28), ease: 'power2.in', at: 2.3 });\n  });\n\n  shot('Prompt', 2.6, 3.2, { enter: { from: 'up' }, exit: 'whip' }, (s) => {\n    // camera push: the only move allowed past 1 s\n    push(s, { target: '.composer', amount: 0.08, frames: f(1) });\n    // typing at 2-3x real speed: 19 characters in 0.8 s\n    type('.typed', 'cut the long pauses', { cps: 24, at: 0.6 });\n  });\n\n  shot('End', 5.8, 2.2, { enter: { from: 'up' } }, () => {\n    // last beat holds to the end, no exit; it rises from the cut frame, so the cut never shows an empty frame\n    reveal('.logo', { from: { y: 0.3 }, frames: f(0.35), ease: 'power3.out' });\n  });\n}\n```\n\n## Pages\n- `ideation`: the conversation before a new film: message, tension, angles.\n- `backgrounds`: the three-layer background built from the brand kit.\n- `brand-kit`: find, create and confirm the brand kit; `brand` in scene code.\n- `blueprints`: rebuild a product screenshot as live HTML with named parts, and when a shot uses it instead of the screenshot.\n- `image-plates`: when and how to generate image assets (photos, textures, cut-outs).\n- `shot-changes`: hard cuts, `whip()` and carry-and-overlap arrivals between shots.\n"
  },
  "launch-films-motions/references/backgrounds.md": {
    "name": "backgrounds",
    "description": "Builds a film background from three layers (base, structure, accent) with colors derived from the brand kit, plus shadow values, banned looks and the measured check. Read before the first addFilm of a new film and before restyling a film's background.",
    "body": "# Backgrounds\n\nA good background is light, quiet and built for this film: one base, at most one structure and one accent, every color derived from the brand kit, and every layer doing a job in the motion.\n\nThe user's explicit direction and a measured recreation override this page. The limits under Rules still apply unless the user explicitly overrides a limit too.\n\nContents: Workflow; Defaults (colors, base, structure, accent, shadows); Rules (letting the user pick, color, every layer does a job, shadows, banned, check before handback).\n\n## Workflow\n1. Read the kit with getBrandKit and derive the colors (Defaults, Colors). With no kit, use the example column.\n2. Choose one base, then zero or one structure, then zero or one accent. When the user has not set a look, let them pick from previews instead (Rules, Letting the user pick).\n3. Write the choice in one line and save it with updateBrief before the first addFilm, for example: \"Base warm paper #F7F5F1, structure none, accent bloom #DDD3F7 behind each headline, end card dark #07060B with corner glows.\"\n4. Build it, then run the check under Rules before handing back.\n\n## Defaults\n\n### Colors\n`mix(a, b, t)` is a straight sRGB mix: t = 0 is a, t = 1 is b.\n\n| Role | Rule | Revline kit example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Base | Kit background when it is light (every channel ≥ #E8); otherwise warm paper #F7F5F1 | #F7F5F1 (kit background #0B0C0E is dark) |\n| Ink | Kit foreground when it is dark (every channel ≤ #30), otherwise #16141A | #16141A |\n| Accent | Kit accent, unchanged | #7C3AED |\n| Bloom | mix(base, accent, 0.18–0.22) | ≈ #DDD3F7 |\n| Tinted canvas | mix(#FFFFFF, accent, 0.10–0.14), then pull 4% toward grey | ≈ #E7E3F4 |\n| Grid lines / dots | base darkened 6–8% (lines), 12–15% (dots) | #ECECEC lines, #D9D9D9 dots |\n\n### Layer 1: base (exactly one)\nUse warm paper #F7F5F1. Switch only when a row below fits better.\n\n| Base | Value | Use instead when |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cool white | #F7F8FD | The subject is technical or the film is heavy on product UI |\n| Clean white | #FFFFFF or #FCFEFB | Colorful footage, photos or tiles carry the color |\n| Tinted canvas | ≈ #E7E3F4 (formula above) | One calm on-brand beat, or a whole quiet film |\n| Dark | #07060B | The end card, or one punch beat |\n\n### Layer 2: structure (zero or one)\nUse none. Add one when the motion needs something to build on or travel over.\n\n| Structure | Spec | Use when |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cell grid | 1 px lines every 48–96 px in the grid-line color | A headline should sit on a cell-aligned box filled with the base color |\n| Crosshair marks (with the cell grid) | 12 px \"+\" marks, 1.5 px stroke in the accent, on the four corners of the focal box | The focal box moves; the marks track it |\n| Dot grid | 2 px dots every 24 px in the dot color | The section has dense UI |\n| Perspective floor | Cell grid rotated about 60–65° on X from the bottom edge, masked out by 60% of frame height | The camera travels |\n| Cool grid + bloom | Cool white base, lines mix(base, #000, 0.06), plus the bloom | Counts as structure and accent together |\n\n### Layer 3: accent (zero or one)\nUse none, or a bloom when the focal point needs lifting. Switch to another row when the accent should build or mark something.\n\n| Accent | Spec | Use instead when |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bloom | One radial glow, at most 42% × 55% of the frame, centered on the focal point: `radial-gradient(42% 55% at X Y, BLOOM 0%, BLOOM@55% 40%, transparent 75%)` over the base | (default) |\n| Pixel field | Square cells 8–16 px, 2 px gaps, accent at 10–100% opacity, from a seeded hash | Cells should build the next panel or word |\n| Pixel bleed | The pixel field spilling across a grid from one corner or edge, gone by 45% of the frame | A grid needs one lively edge |\n| Lit cells | Whole grid cells filled mix(base, accent, 0.08); one may use 0.16 | Cells should mark the beat, like a step sequencer |\n\n### Shadows\n| Element | Shadow |\n|---|---|\n| Card, tile or window on a light base (dark UI objects too) | `0 18px 40px rgba(20,10,40,.10), 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,.06)` |\n| Lifted or selected element (the darkest allowed) | `0 24px 56px rgba(20,10,40,.14)` |\n\n## Rules\n\n### Letting the user pick\nBefore the first addFilm, when the request, the brief and any reference video set no look, preview 3–5 candidates with previewBackgrounds and let the user pick (its description gives the askUser step):\n- Each pair differs in at least one layer (base, structure or accent).\n- At most one uses a dark base, and only for an end-card or punch-beat film.\n- Give each a 2–5 word label and a one-line note on what it suits.\n- Save the pick's returned line with updateBrief (rule: the line; userWords: what the user said); the tool never writes the brief itself.\n- On feedback such as \"2 but warmer\" or \"1 with a grid\", preview 3 variations of that candidate that change only the named layer (warmer: base #F7F5F1 → #F5F0E8 → #F2EBDF), then ask again. Build only once a pick lands.\n\nSkip the preview when the user named a look, the brief already holds a background line, or the film recreates a reference.\n\n### Color\n- A dark kit background is the product's UI color, not the film canvas.\n- When the user asks for a color (\"make it orange\", \"go dark\"), replace only that role and recompute the roles derived from it; keep every limit.\n- The body of a launch film is light: a base between #F7F5F1 and #FFFFFF, near-black ink and one saturated accent.\n- Dark is for the end card or one punch beat, never the body. Dark product UI sits on the light base as an object.\n- The accent color appears in one layer. Headlines use the ink color.\n- A grid that reads as a pattern from across the room is too strong; the check below measures it.\n\n### Every layer does a job\n- A grid builds itself, carries the focal box, or gives the camera something to travel over.\n- A bloom follows the focal point between sections, with the same ease as the camera.\n- A pixel field builds things: cells fill the area where a panel or word will appear, staggered 3–5 ms per cell; the real element resolves over them and the cells fade out within 0.4 s.\n- Lit cells switch on with the beat.\n- A layer that only sits there is removed.\n- Change the background at most once per act (opening, middle, end card), never per beat.\n- Carry layers through a transition (the grid scrolls, the bloom slides) instead of crossfading two backgrounds.\n\n### Shadows\n- No shadows on text over a light base.\n\n### Banned\n- Full-frame mesh or multi-stop gradients.\n- Film grain on a dark canvas.\n- Neon, glowing orbs, blue-tinted darks (#1D202A style), heavy black drop shadows.\n- A solid fill in the bright brand accent on a card, pill or the whole frame.\n\n### Check before handback\ngetFrames on two body beats and the end card, then measureFrame the base in an empty corner of each. Fail and fix when:\n- a body beat's base is darker than #E0E0E0 (unless the user asked for dark),\n- grid or dot contrast against the base is above 8% (measureFrame both colors),\n- the accent covers more than about 15% of a frame outside a pixel build in progress.\n"
  },
  "launch-films-motions/references/blueprints.md": {
    "name": "blueprints",
    "description": "Rebuilds a product screenshot as a pixel-perfect blueprint (live HTML with named parts), scores it against the screenshot, saves it to the brand kit and mounts it in a film. Read when a film shows the user's product UI: it decides per shot between the screenshot and a blueprint, and builds a blueprint when the kit has none for that screen.",
    "body": "# Blueprints: screenshot to pixel-perfect HTML\n\nA good blueprint is a kit screen rebuilt as a responsive scene module: every piece (header, sidebar, composer, tab, button) is HTML with a `data-part` name, measured from the screen's pixels and scoring 95%+ against it. Once saved in the kit, every film for that brand can mount it.\n\n## Workflow\n1. **Choose per shot** between the screenshot and a blueprint (Rules). For a blueprint, check listBlueprints first. If the kit has one for that screen, use it and skip to step 8.\n2. **Source.** Use an untouched capture from the kit (getBrandKit screens). If the only capture is edited, ask for a new one: painting over a badge or cursor also erases what was under it.\n3. **Measure** with measureScreen: a row across the header, a column down the sidebar, both axes through each button. Read every box's width, height and corner radius, and build at the screen's size from those pixels.\n4. **Icons.** Match a few icons by shape to Lucide names and use `icon(...)`. Brand logos and app icons come from findIcon; other icons Lucide lacks from findIcon style ui. Draw an SVG only for what neither has.\n5. **Build** a module that takes `{ root, width, height, brand, media, icon }` and gives every meaningful piece `data-part`.\n6. **Compare** with compareScreen at the screen's size. Fix the lowest-scoring parts, compare again.\n7. **Save** with saveBlueprint once it scores 95%+ overall with no flat part (button, panel, divider) under 90%.\n8. **Mount** it in the film (Example below).\n\n## Rules\n- **Screenshot or blueprint, per shot:** when the whole UI moves or holds, use the screenshot (`media.<screen>`). When something inside the UI moves, zooms past 2× or changes text, use the kit's blueprint.\n- Fonts and the logo come from the kit; colors and positions come from the screen. Never type a size, color or radius by eye.\n- Snap a measured color to the kit's palette only when they are nearly identical.\n- Lay the UI out responsively: fixed parts keep their measured size (a sidebar 400 px wide, a header 56 px tall), stretching regions take the rest, right-hand items anchor right, centered things stay centered.\n- Photos inside the UI (footage, avatars) may be cropped from the screen media.\n- Read per-part scores, not only the total: a 96% screen can hide a part at 0%. Judge text-heavy parts by the diff image.\n- Check desktop (1920x1080) and laptop (1440x900) with compareScreen width and height when the kit has captures at those sizes. Otherwise say those sizes are unverified.\n- Attach it with `brandKitId` and `blueprints: ['Editor']` on addFilm, addScene or editScene. Scale or tilt the container to frame it.\n- The blueprint stays static; its motion is written in the film's sections.\n\n## Example\n```ts\nconst ui = blueprint('Editor', root.querySelector('.device'), { width: 1600, height: 1000 });\nsection('Composer', 4, 3, (s) => {\n  s.from(ui.querySelector('[data-part=composer]'), { y: 40, opacity: 0, duration: .35, ease: 'power3.out' }, .2);\n});\n```\n"
  },
  "launch-films-motions/references/brand-kit.md": {
    "name": "brand-kit",
    "description": "Finds, creates and confirms the user's brand kit (fonts, colors, logo, screens, product text) and documents the `brand` object films receive. Read first for a new film for the user's own brand; not used when recreating a reference.",
    "body": "# Brand kit\n\nA good kit is confirmed by the user: the right fonts by role, the main colors, the logo, and (only when needed) a confirmed product text and saved product screens. Films attach it with `brandKitId` and receive it as `brand`.\n\n## Workflow\n1. **Check first.** listBrandKits.\n   - One kit clearly fits: ask \"Use the <name> brand kit for this film?\" and wait.\n   - Several fit: name them and ask which.\n   - None: ask for the website URL. If there is no site, ask for a theme (colors, Google Fonts names, logo SVG).\n2. **Create.** createBrandKit with the URL and any theme values the user gave (those win). Name it after the brand.\n3. **Show what was found:** fonts by role, the main colors, whether a logo was found, and every note. Ask the user to correct anything wrong; fix it with updateBrandKit.\n4. **Confirm the product**, only when the film states product facts the user has not given (step details under Rules).\n5. **Screens**, only when the user wants their product UI shown. Check the kit's screens with getBrandKit.\n6. **Use.** Pass `brandKitId` to addFilm or addScene, or to editScene to attach or refresh it.\n\n## Rules\n- Never pretend a missing piece was found.\n- **Product text:** draft one from the scraped copy: what it is, the problem it solves and 3 to 5 features worth showing, as short lines.\n- Show it and ask the user to correct it, asking only about what the copy does not answer. Save the confirmed text with updateBrandKit context.product.\n- Never build on an unconfirmed product text.\n- **Screens belong to the brand, not one project.** When the user gives screenshots, offer to save them to the kit (updateBrandKit addScreens, named like editor, dashboard, settings, with a short note) so later films reuse them.\n\n## In scene code\n`brand` is null without a kit. With one:\n- `brand.fonts.heading`, `brand.fonts.body`: loaded family names.\n- `brand.colors`: named colors. Read the names with getBrandKit first and fall back when one is missing. `brand.palette` lists them in order.\n- `brand.logo`: a data URL for `<img src>`. `brand.logoSvg`: the SVG markup when the logo is vector; insert it to animate its paths.\n- Kit screens arrive in `media` by name (`media.editor`), loaded like project media. A project image attached with the same name wins.\n\n## Example\n```ts\nconst heading = brand?.fonts.heading ?? 'Inter';   // fall back when there is no kit\nroot.innerHTML = `<div class=\"logo\">${brand?.logoSvg ?? ''}</div><h1 style=\"font-family:'${heading}'\">Ship faster</h1>`;\n```\n"
  },
  "launch-films-motions/references/ideation.md": {
    "name": "ideation",
    "description": "Runs the short conversation before a new film: one message, one audience tension and 3–5 pictured angles with hooks, ending the moment the user accepts an angle. Read before building a new film; skip it for small edits or \"just build it\".",
    "body": "# Ideation: talk, then build\n\nA good ideation round is one message from the agent: what is already known, at most 2–3 questions with defaults, a one-sentence message, a tension and 3–5 angles. When the user accepts an angle, building starts.\n\n## Workflow\n1. **Read what is known:** readBrief, inspectTimeline and the matching getBrandKit. Reuse the known audience, product, platform and aspect ratio, length, audio and ending.\n2. **Ask** at most 2–3 missing questions, in one message, each with a default.\n3. **Propose the message** in one plain sentence: what the viewer should believe.\n4. **State the tension** in one sentence the audience recognises.\n5. **Offer 3–5 angles** as short bullets, each one visible action plus a \"Hooks because …\" line of at most 15 words.\n6. **Pick one** bold angle of your own and say why you like it.\n7. **Build** as soon as the user accepts an angle.\n\n## Rules\n\n### Questions\n- Without a stated length, suggest one sized to the content (\"about 20 s: title, three features, end card\"). An explicit length or \"no logo\" wins.\n- Answer the user's current question in the first 1–2 sentences. Build on their ideas rather than repeating a ballot.\n- Use one question to resolve the next uncertainty, for example \"Is sameness the frustration you want to attack?\"\n- When the user asks what you have in mind, describe the proposed beats, exact copy, sound and setting in plain words.\n\n### Message and claims\n- Bad message: \"Make the video you imagined without juggling tools.\"\n- Good message, if the user confirmed that claim: \"Editing software hasn't changed in 7 years. Revline is the first thing that feels different.\"\n- Numbers and product claims follow launch-films-motions, Never speculate: check them against readBrief, or ask once.\n\n### Metaphors\n- Develop the meaning under a metaphor, not the metaphor itself.\n- User: \"Throw it in the trash.\" Bad: bin → compactor → rocket bin.\n- Good: \"Do you mean the old tools, or repeating the same edit? If it's repetition, I'd show seven identical timelines, then break that pattern.\"\n- If their meaning is already clear, develop it without asking again.\n\n### Acceptance\n- \"Do it\", \"go with that\", \"yes\" and \"that one\" accept the angle they answer. The conversation ends and building starts, with no intermediate review rounds.\n- Example: the agent proposes the factory; the user says \"do it\" → start the film code, not two more factory choices.\n- Ask one clarifying question only if no angle is identifiable.\n- No saved planning stage is required to build.\n\n## Example\nTension: \"Every new editor still makes you repeat the same trim.\"\n- Seven desks replay the same trim; the eighth breaks the loop. Hooks because editors recognise the repetition before the product appears.\n- A factory stamps identical timelines; one edit refuses the stamp. Hooks because the broken pattern makes the difference visible.\n- One manual cut stays fixed while the surrounding edit changes. Hooks because it answers the fear of losing work to automation.\n\n\"I'd lead with the factory: you see the break before reading anything.\"\n"
  },
  "launch-films-motions/references/image-plates.md": {
    "name": "image-plates",
    "description": "Decides when a film needs a generated image asset (photoreal subject, texture, background plate, cut-out) and how to brief, check and attach it. Read before calling generateImage.",
    "body": "# Image plates\n\nA good plate is one useful asset that code could not draw, in the film's palette, with no text, framed for its exact use and checked before it is placed.\n\n## Workflow\n1. **Decide:** generate only what neither code nor findIcon or traceShape can make (generateImage lists that order): hands, physical objects, products, photos, textures, backgrounds and cut-outs.\n2. **Brief** one asset with the seven prompt items (Rules, Prompt).\n3. **Generate** one variant first. Ask for alternatives only when they are useful.\n4. **Inspect** the preview: subject, anatomy, edges, framing, palette and unwanted text.\n5. **Correct** a failed asset once with a sharper brief, passing its asset ID in referenceAssetIds; never generate a series of guesses.\n6. **Attach** it as scene media (writing-scenes, Project media) and place it (Example).\n\n## Rules\n\n### Prompt\n- Give seven concrete items: medium and format, one reference era or discipline, composition and framing, lighting, 2–4 named colours, texture and material, exclusions.\n- Use the film's actual palette for backgrounds, textures and image fills.\n- Add \"no text, no lettering, no logos\" for assets that need none; set final type in code.\n- An isolated subject keeps room around its silhouette. A texture or backdrop states its coverage and crop.\n- A texture states its material, scale and seamless coverage; inspect the seams before repeating it.\n- An environment-map plate asks for a panoramic reflection and lighting reference; inspect its projection.\n\n### Format\n- Use transparentBackground: true for a subject composited over a film. Ask for no background or floor, and keep the whole silhouette in frame.\n- Use an opaque plate (the default) for a photo, texture or background.\n- For an edit or variation, say what to preserve and exactly what to change.\n\n### Banned unless requested\n- Neon or glow, glassmorphism, purple or blue gradients, bright purple fills, glowing orbs, lens flares, sparkles, holograms, stock futuristic decoration, AI-looking skin.\n- A muted grain gradient is allowed as an image fill when the user asks for it. Film backgrounds follow page `backgrounds`.\n\n### Recreations\n- A reference supplies framing, palette, material and motion observations. Describe a fresh subject or material from them.\n- Never generate, edit or upload reproductions of its frames, whole compositions, UI or typography, and never pass an extracted reference frame as a reference image.\n- Rebuild those layers in code and generate only the assets they need.\n\n### Placement\n- Give the `<img>` explicit width and height and `object-fit: contain`.\n- Keep text, shapes and backgrounds as separate scene elements.\n\n## Example\nOpaque plate: \"Studio product photograph, 1980s industrial-design catalogue. One retro beige desktop computer, three-quarter front view, complete monitor and keyboard centered with generous margins. Ivory, charcoal and warm grey; matte aged plastic and fine 35mm grain. Large softbox above left, solid cream background, blank dark screen. No text, lettering, logos, flares or extra objects.\"\n\nCut-out (transparentBackground: true): \"35mm studio close-up, documentary still-life discipline. One adult hand holding a blank ivory card, wrist entering from below, three-quarter front view, whole silhouette with generous clear margins. Natural umber, ivory and warm grey; real skin creases, matte paper, subtle grain. Broad soft side light, transparent background. No floor, backdrop, text, lettering, logos, jewelry or beauty retouching.\"\n\n```ts\n// attached as media: [{ name: 'hand', assetId: '<returned assetId>' }]\nroot.insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend', `<img class=\"hand\" src=\"${media.hand}\" style=\"position:absolute;left:55%;top:30%;width:520px;height:520px;object-fit:contain\">`);\n```\n"
  },
  "launch-films-motions/references/shot-changes.md": {
    "name": "shot-changes",
    "description": "How a film changes shots: hard cut by default, the whip() helper (left, right, up, down, zoom, zoom-out; 4–5 frames), arrivals that carry in and overlap the exit before them, and the frame checks. Read before any cut, whip or arrival between sections of a film.",
    "body": "# Shot changes in films\n\nA good shot change in a film is a hard cut, unless a chosen beat earns a whip. A whip is 4–5 frames: the shots flick a short way in one direction with motion blur while both stay composed where they belong. Nothing slides in from off-screen, and every arrival is already moving when it appears.\n\nTerms: the **cut** is the first frame of the incoming shot; `f` is one frame (1/30 s at 30 fps); **travel** is how far the shot moves, as a share of the frame's width (left/right) or height (up/down).\n\nFootage (reels, clips, montages) changes shots with addTransition in the `transitions` skill, not with this page.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Pick each shot change: a hard cut, or a whip where When to whip allows.\n2. Compose both shots at rest first (Rules, Composition).\n3. Call `whip()` for each whip (Film code). Never hand-write a whip: `whip()` and the footage whip-pan share one motion.\n4. Time every arrival by Rules (Carry and overlap), whip or not.\n5. Check with getFrames as Check lists.\n\n## Defaults\nAt 30 fps. A reference being copied and the user's direction win.\n\n| Shot change | Frames | Seconds | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hard cut | 0 | 0 | Every shot change by default |\n| Whip (left, right, up, down, zoom, zoom-out) | 4–5 | 0.13–0.17 | A topic change or a chosen music hit |\n| Fade through black | 9 out + 9 in | 0.3 + 0.3 | The film's end only |\n\n### Whip settings\n| Whip | Travel | Outgoing shot (2 frames) | Incoming shot (2 frames) | Peak blur at 1080p |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Left | 15% of width (10–25%) | x 0 → −15%, `power4.in` | x +15% → 0, `expo.out` | 24 px, horizontal only |\n| Right | 15% of width (10–25%) | x 0 → +15%, `power4.in` | x −15% → 0, `expo.out` | 24 px, horizontal only |\n| Up | 15% of height (10–25%) | y 0 → −15%, `power4.in` | y +15% → 0, `expo.out` | 24 px, vertical only |\n| Down | 15% of height (10–25%) | y 0 → +15%, `power4.in` | y −15% → 0, `expo.out` | 24 px, vertical only |\n| Zoom (in) | scale 1.25 | scale 1 → 1.25, `power4.in` | scale 1.12 → 1, `expo.out` | 10 px |\n| Zoom out | scale 0.8 | scale 1 → 0.8, `power4.in` | scale 0.88 → 1, `expo.out` | 10 px |\n\nThe direction is where the picture travels on screen: in a whip left, both shots move toward the left edge.\n\n### Frame by frame (whip left, cut at T)\n| Frame | Shown | Frame offset | Blur |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T − 3f | outgoing, at rest | 0 | 0 |\n| T − 2f | outgoing | −0.9% | 1.5 px |\n| T − f | outgoing, peak | −15% | 24 px |\n| T | incoming, peak | +15% | 24 px |\n| T + f | incoming | +0.5% | 0.8 px |\n| T + 2f | incoming, at rest | 0 | 0 |\n\n## Rules\n\n### When to whip\n- Whip only on a topic change or a music hit you chose. Check: at most one shot change in three is a whip, and never two in a row.\n- Never whip while a camera move is still running through the cut, or against the motion in the shots.\n\n### Composition\n- **Never park a part off-center so it can slide.** Wrong: the product card sits at `left:70%` and slides to the center as the whip. Right: the card sits centered in both shots and `whip()` flicks the whole shot.\n- **Whip whole shots, not single parts:** `out` and `in` are the shot containers (the full-frame layer each section shows), not one card inside them.\n- **No empty edge:** the background sits on the stage, outside the shots, so the travel shows background, not a gap. A shot with its own full-frame background puts it on a layer with `inset:-25%`.\n- **The whip is the exit and the entrance:** the outgoing shot runs no exit move in its last 3 frames, and the incoming shot runs no entrance in its first 2.\n- **One frame clock:** the incoming shot arrives already moving in the whip's direction and settles; it never stops at the cut and starts again.\n\n### Carry and overlap\nApplies to every arrival after a hard cut or an exit, not only whips.\n- **Overlap:** the incoming shot's entrance starts 2–4 frames (0.07–0.13 s) before the outgoing shot has fully left. Wrong: the title's exit ends at 2.58 s and the card's entrance starts at 2.7 s, leaving a frame with nothing moving. Right: the card starts at 2.5 s.\n- **Arrive moving:** the first frame the incoming part is visible, it is already offset and moving, never parked at its start position for a frame.\n- **Land fast-start, slow-end:** `expo.out` or `power4.out`, 0.35–0.5 s. Never an inOut ease, which starts slow and reads as a predictable zoom in, zoom out.\n- **A hold after the landing is fine:** once the arrival has settled, the shot may rest for its beat. The rule is only that the arrival itself is not a dead stop or a frozen frame.\n- Overlapping moves span two sections, so they go on the outer `tl` (Film code, Carry and overlap).\n\n## Film code\n\n### whip()\nScene code receives `whip` next to `section`. It runs the recipe in Defaults: the outgoing shot accelerates out (`power4.in`), the incoming one arrives already moving the same way and settles (`expo.out`), and the blur along the axis peaks at the cut and is 0 on the last frame. Shots stay where they are composed. The motion is the same as the footage whip-pan's, frame for frame.\n\n`whip(tl, { out, in, at, direction, frames, travel, blur, fill })`\n\n| Option | Values | Default |\n|---|---|---|\n| `tl` | the scene's `tl` (at in film seconds) or the `tl` a section receives (at in section seconds) | |\n| `out`, `in` | an element, a selector inside root, or a list | |\n| `at` | the cut: the first frame of `in` | |\n| `direction` | `left`, `right`, `up`, `down`, `zoom`, `zoom-out` | `left` |\n| `frames` | 4 or 5 (Defaults), counted at 30 fps; 3 or 6 only to match a reference | 4 |\n| `travel` | 0.1–0.25 of the frame (zoom: 0.15 scales to 1.25) | 0.15 |\n| `blur` | `true`, `false`, or peak px at 1080p (0–48) | `true`: 24 px, zooms 10 px |\n| `fill` | `true` keeps the other shot visible underneath, filling the edge the front one uncovers, as footage does; only for opaque full-frame shots | `false` |\n\n- Hide `in` in its markup (`visibility:hidden`); whip() shows it at the cut (with `fill`, from the whip's first frame), hides `out` from the cut (with `fill`, after the last frame), and leaves both at rest after the whip, so a later cut back shows them in place.\n- The whip must fit in the film: a few frames before the cut, and its incoming frames before the film ends.\n- Called inside a section, the whip may run past that section's end: it goes on the film's timeline.\n- Shots are `position:absolute` layers in a positioned parent (the stage). Hide a shot with visibility or opacity, never `display:none`.\n- It wraps each shot once in a full-size layer (`[data-whip-layer]`) and moves and blurs only that layer, so the shots' own GSAP moves and filters keep working. Select shots by class, not `.stage > .shot`.\n\n```js\nexport default function scene({ root, section, whip }) {\n  root.innerHTML = `\n    <div class=\"stage\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;overflow:hidden;background:#141413;color:#F4F4F0\">\n      <div class=\"hook\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;display:grid;place-items:center;font:600 120px Inter\">Cut the pauses</div>\n      <div class=\"product\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;display:grid;place-items:center;visibility:hidden\">\n        <div style=\"width:960px;height:540px;border-radius:32px;background:#2a2a2a\"></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"end\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;display:grid;place-items:center;font:700 140px Inter;visibility:hidden\">Revline</div>\n    </div>`;\n  section('Hook', 0, 2, (s) => {\n    whip(s, { out: '.hook', in: '.product', at: 2, direction: 'right' });   // topic change at 2 s\n  });\n  section('Product', 2, 2.5, (s) => {\n    whip(s, { out: '.product', in: '.end', at: 2.5, direction: 'zoom' });\n  });\n  section('End', 4.5, 2, () => {});\n}\n```\n\n### Carry and overlap\n```js\n// Hook ends at 2.6 s: its title leaves 2.3–2.58 s inside its section.\nsection('Hook', 0, 2.6, (s) => {\n  s.to($('.title'), { y: -30, opacity: 0, duration: 0.28, ease: 'power2.in' }, 2.3);\n});\nsection('Product', 2.6, 3, () => {});\n// The card starts 0.08 s (about 2 frames) before the title has left, already moving, and lands on expo.out.\ntl.from($('.card'), { y: 60, opacity: 0, duration: 0.45, ease: 'expo.out' }, 2.5);\n// It then holds, settled, for the rest of the beat.\n```\n\n## Check\n- getFrames rounds a time down to its frame, so ask 0.5 ms after each frame: for a cut at 6 s, 5.9675 (T − f), 6.0005 (T) and 6.0675 (T + 2f).\n- getFrames at T − f and T: both frames offset about 15% the same way, smeared along the whip axis only (zoom: scaled, softened).\n- getFrames at T + 2f: the incoming shot sharp, centered and at rest.\n- getFrames at T − 3f: the outgoing shot at rest, still composed where it belongs.\n- Carry and overlap: getFrames 0.5 ms after the incoming part's first visible frame and one frame later: it has moved between them, and the outgoing part is still visible in the first.\n- Count the shot changes: at most one in three is a whip.\n\n## Example\nA 12 s film with four shot changes; the topic changes at 6 s.\n- 3 s, 9 s, 10.5 s: hard cuts, each next part arriving 2–4 frames before the last one has left.\n- 6 s: whip left, `whip(tl, { out: '.shot-b', in: '.shot-c', at: 6 })`, both shots centered where they were composed.\n- getFrames at 5.9675 and 6.0005 shows both shots shifted left and smeared horizontally; at 6.0675 the new shot is sharp and centered.\n"
  },
  "music/SKILL.md": {
    "name": "music",
    "description": "Puts a song under an edit: where the song starts (its strongest moment), cutting on downbeats, beats or accents, edit length in whole 4/8-bar phrases, ending on a phrase end, and ducking under voice. Read before placing, trimming or cutting to any song, in every workflow.",
    "body": "# Music\n\nA good music edit opens where the song gets good, cuts land on the song's own bar lines, the edit lasts whole phrases and ends where the music ends a phrase, and any voice stays on top. Every musical time comes from analyzeMusic, never from ear-guessing, a loudness scan by hand or bar maths.\n\nTerms: a **beat** is one pulse (0.5 s at 120 BPM); a **bar** is beatsPerBar beats starting on a **downbeat** (2 s at 120 BPM in 4/4); a **phrase** is 4 or 8 bars (8 or 16 s at 120 BPM); a **moment** is a downbeat where analyzeMusic measured the energy arriving.\n\n## Workflow\n1. inspectTimeline: find the song's audio clip ID (or its asset ID in media if it is not placed yet; place it with addMedia).\n2. analyzeMusic { clipId, lengths: [the target length] }: tempo, bars, sections, ranked moments and the best windows for that length. The first call measures the song (about 15–30 s); later calls read the saved result.\n3. startMusicAt { clipId, moment: 1, length }: the song starts on moment 1's downbeat and lasts whole bars. Use sourceTime instead of moment for a window start or a user-named time.\n4. Size each shot in whole bars or beats (Cutting), then alignCutsToBeat { musicAssetId } snaps every V1 cut to the nearest beat, which is the downbeat when the shot was sized in bars. Load skill music with loadCapability for alignCutsToBeat and markMusicBeats when they are not loaded yet.\n5. Under voice, set the song's role and ducking with configureAudio (Voice).\n6. Listen: getSound from 2 s before to 4 s after the song's timeline start, and over the last 4 s (Checks).\n7. finish.\n\n## Choosing the start\n| Request | Start |\n| --- | --- |\n| No start named (default) | moment 1, the strongest: `startMusicAt { moment: 1 }` |\n| \"From the start\", \"with the intro\" | Source 0, or the first downbeat when the song opens with a pickup |\n| A visual payoff at timeline T (a reveal, a kill, a logo) | The moment minus T, in whole bars, so the moment lands on the payoff: `startMusicAt { sourceTime: moment.time - T }` snaps to the nearest downbeat |\n| A user-named time (\"start at the chorus\", \"at 2:43\") | `startMusicAt { sourceTime: 163 }`; it snaps to the nearest downbeat |\n| A target length | The top window for that length: `startMusicAt { sourceTime: window.start, length }` |\n| A recreation with matchMusic | Keep the start matchMusic chose: it follows the reference's energy and downbeats |\n\n- Prefer a moment with a sung line (`sung`) when two moments score within 0.1 of each other: a recognisable hook beats a slightly louder bar.\n- A moment with jumpDb under 3 and lowJumpDb under 6 is not a real arrival; say the song stays level and start on the top window instead.\n- Times in analyzeMusic are SOURCE seconds; `timeline` and `inClip` give where they play now. Never mix the two.\n\n## Cutting\n| Cut on | When | Shot length at 120 BPM |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Downbeats (bar lines) | Default for every scene or shot change; section changes always | 1, 2 or 4 bars: 2, 4 or 8 s |\n| Beats | Fast montage shots of 1–2 beats, only inside a loud section (peak or drop) | 0.5–1 s |\n| Accents | Not cuts: flashes, zooms or shakes on strong hits (strength 0.8 or more), at most 1 per bar | an instant |\n\n- Change pace at section lines: hold longer shots (2–4 bars) in low and build sections, cut faster (1 bar or beats) in peak and drop sections.\n- The biggest picture change (the reveal, the first action shot) goes on moment 1's downbeat.\n- A cut is on the beat when it sits within 1 frame of it; alignCutsToBeat does this for V1. A film made with addFilm is one clip: move its section() start times to the downbeat times in code instead.\n- Accents for effects come from markMusicBeats { kind: \"accents\" } or setAudioReactive mode accents, never from every beat.\n\n## Length and ending\n- Edit length is whole phrases: 4, 8 or 16 bars. At 128 BPM a bar is 1.875 s, so 8 bars = 15 s and 16 bars = 30 s. analyzeMusic returns `phraseSeconds` and windows already in whole bars.\n- When the requested length is not a whole phrase, use the phrase nearest to it and say so (\"16 bars is 28.7 s, closest to the 30 s you asked for\"), unless the user fixed the length exactly; then end on a downbeat and fade out (below).\n- End on a section end or a phrase end (the window's `endsOn`). A hard ending on a section end needs no fade.\n- An ending mid-section gets a fade out of 1–2 bars (2–4 s at 120 BPM) with configureAudio fadeOut. Never cut a song off mid-bar.\n- The last picture cut and the song's end land on the same downbeat.\n\n## Voice\n- Song under speech or narration: configureAudio { role: \"music\", ducking: { enabled: true, amount: 0.65 } } (about 9 dB lower under the voice, attack 0.12 s, release 0.25 s), and the voice clip labelled role voice.\n- Music volume under a voice starts at 0.25–0.35; a music-only edit keeps volume 1.\n- Keep moment 1 off spoken words where possible: a drop under a sentence masks it. Move the moment into a pause, or start one bar later.\n\n## Checks\n- getSound around the start: the onsets and momentary loudness rise within 0.1 s of the song's timeline start, by about the moment's jumpDb. If the rise is a bar early or late, the clip moved: run startMusicAt again.\n- getSound over the last 4 s: the song ends on its last bar, with no sound cut mid-onset (a fade or a section end).\n- With voice: getSound over a spoken stretch shows the words, and no music onset is louder than the voice level there.\n- Report the start the way a person hears it: \"The song starts on its chorus at 2:43 (bar 81, +7.9 dB, 'I'm beggin', beggin' you')\", not the tool's raw numbers.\n\n## Example\n\"Put Beggin' under this 30 s montage\" (a 134 BPM song on A1, montage of 9 shots on V1):\n\n```\ninspectTimeline                                   → song clip a1 (asset s1), V1 shots\nanalyzeMusic { clipId: \"a1\", lengths: [30] }      → bar 1.791 s; moment 1 at 163.2 s, bar 92, +7.9 dB, low band +9.4 dB,\n                                                     sung \"I'm beggin', beggin' you\"; window 30: 163.2–191.9 s, 16 bars, ends a section\nstartMusicAt { clipId: \"a1\", moment: 1, length: 30 } → source 163.2–191.86 s, 16 bars, timeline 0–28.66 s\nalignCutsToBeat { musicAssetId: \"s1\" }            → 8 cuts and the end snapped to downbeats\ngetSound { start: 0, end: 4 }                     → onsets and loudness rise at 0.0 s\ngetSound { start: 24.66, end: 28.66 }             → ends on the section end\nfinish\n```\n\nReply: \"The montage now opens on the chorus at 2:43 ('I'm beggin', beggin' you') and runs 16 bars, 28.7 s, closest to your 30 s, ending where the chorus ends. All 8 cuts land on bar lines.\"\n"
  },
  "remaking-videos/SKILL.md": {
    "name": "remaking-videos",
    "description": "Remakes a video the user shares, or borrows parts of it (a transition, a title style, the pacing, the look) for the user's own edit. Read before analyzing a reference, before a remake, and before borrowing any part of one; recreating a launch or motion film also needs launch-films-motions.",
    "body": "# Remaking videos\n\nThe user shares a finished video and wants either a **remake** (the same edit made with their own material) or a **style borrow** (one or more parts of it applied to their edit). A good result starts with a short, concrete breakdown by shot id, uses measured numbers exactly, and checks every model reading in frames before it decides the build.\n\nFor original work inspired by a reference, follow launch-films-motions \"A style reference\": borrow the reasons behind its choices, never its imagery, layout or timing.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Break it down: run analyzeMedia with mode reference on the shared video.\n2. Read positions, sizes and type from full-size frames: readFrames with crop, and measureFrame for exact colors and edges.\n3. Reply with the breakdown (see Breakdown reply).\n4. Ask, only if the message did not already say: remake it, or borrow which parts?\n5. Load the page for the job (see Defaults) and build.\n6. After building, look at the result with getFrames at the shots that matter, sampled as writing-scenes page `edit-lanes` sets for the lane; a scene remake also closes out with page `recreation`.\n\n## Breakdown reply\n- One line: what it is (genre), its length and shot count, the rhythm (median shot, cuts on the beat, bpm).\n- One line per shot, grouping long runs of similar shots: id, time, transition in, what is on screen by part, title style when there is one. For example: \"S4 (6.2 to 8.1 s): whip right in. Phone UI cropped to the composer, small grotesk headline blurs in, slow push in.\"\n- The signatures by id (T1, T2), in a few words each.\n- Mention once that the chat shows the breakdown as a card where the user can click shots and parts to add them to the next message.\n\n## Defaults\n| Job | Page to load |\n| --- | --- |\n| Remake of footage cut to music, including an ad made of footage | `social-edit` |\n| Remake of a motivation or quote video | `motivation` |\n| Remake of an ad, music video, vlog or tutorial | the closest of `social-edit` and `motivation` |\n| Remake of a launch or motion film | `recreation`, plus the launch-films-motions skill |\n| Borrow named parts | `borrow` |\n\n## Rules\n- The reference stays off the timeline unless the user asks for it there. A file dropped into an empty project lands on the timeline by itself: before building a remake, delete that reference clip from the timeline (it stays in media).\n- Measure at full resolution, never from a downscaled contact sheet: a shrunk sheet makes type and objects look about half their real size.\n- For a footage edit or style borrow, copy the edit using the user's material. For an explicitly requested motion-film copy, follow page `recreation` for exact visible content.\n- For a footage remake, say what comes from the reference and what comes from the user's material.\n- When the remake needs material the project does not have, list it per shot and ask before building (for example \"S1 to S3: a slow establishing shot. S4: fast action on the drop\").\n- Measured numbers (shot lengths, cut times, beats, onBeatRatio) are exact.\n- Transition kinds, camera moves and title styles are the model's reading. When one decides the result, or is unclear in the breakdown, check it with readFrames fps 10 on the reference around that shot's time before building.\n\n## Example\nA user shares a 14 s car edit and says \"make this with my clips\". The reply:\n\n> Car edit, 14.2 s, 9 shots, median shot 1.4 s, cuts on the beat at 128 bpm.\n> - S1 (0 to 2.8 s): hard cut in. Slow establishing pan over the car, no title.\n> - S2 to S6 (2.8 to 8.1 s): detail shots, two beats each, white flash on every cut.\n> - S7 (8.1 to 10.4 s): whip right in. Speed ramp into the drop, shake on the hit.\n> - S8 and S9 (10.4 to 14.2 s): slow push in, bold uppercase title \"NIGHT RUN\" pops in.\n> - Signatures: T1 white flash on the downbeat; T2 speed ramp into the hit.\n>\n> You can click shots and parts in the card to add them to your next message. S7 needs fast action for the drop; your clips have none, can you add one?\n\nThe request already says remake, so nothing else is asked; page `social-edit` is loaded for the build.\n\n## Pages\n- `recreation`: the full motion-film recreation workflow: inventories, exact content, per-layer camera, rendered comparisons, close-out.\n- `social-edit`: remaking footage cut to music, shot by shot on the user's beats.\n- `motivation`: remaking footage under a voice with bold captions.\n- `borrow`: applying one named part of a reference and how each part maps onto tools.\n- `image-plates` (from launch-films-motions): real asset needs during a build.\n"
  },
  "remaking-videos/references/borrow.md": {
    "name": "borrow",
    "description": "Borrows one part of a reference (a transition, a title style, the pacing, the look) for the current edit and maps each part onto controls.",
    "body": "# Borrow a style\n\nA good borrow changes only what the user named; everything else stays as it was (writing-scenes page `edit-lanes`, Direction and feedback).\n\n## Workflow\n1. Resolve what the user pointed at with inspectReference before building, whether named by id (S4, S4.title, T2) or in words (\"the second transition\", \"the title at 0:07\", \"that zoom\").\n2. Say what was understood, by id (\"S4's whip right, 0.35 s\").\n3. Apply the part with the tools in Defaults, only where the user asked.\n\n## Defaults\n\n### Parts\n| Part | Apply with |\n| --- | --- |\n| Transition | addTransition with the closest preset (table below) and the measured seconds, on the cut the user named, or on every cut if they said so |\n| Title style on text clips | setTextStyle, setTextAnimation or applyTextLook with the measured size, weight, case and enter |\n| Caption style | styleCaptions |\n| Title inside a scene | editScene, changing only the title's CSS and GSAP |\n| Camera move on footage | configureCameraMotion or configureSmoothZoom |\n| Camera move inside a scene | the scene's camera timeline |\n| Pacing | trim and alignCutsToBeat (see Rules) |\n| Look | grade with applyLut, setColorCurves or matchColor; texture with grain or vignette effects |\n| Signature (T1, T2) | its recipe wherever the user asked, with the shots it lists as the model |\n\n### Transition presets\n| Reference kind | addTransition preset |\n| --- | --- |\n| whip | whip-pan, whipDirection as the reference moves (left, right, up, down, zoom, zoom-out) |\n| zoom | zoom-through |\n| dissolve | crossfade |\n| blur | blur-dissolve |\n| spin | spin-zoom |\n| wipe | gradient-wipe (iris-wipe for a circle) |\n| light-leak | film-burn |\n| flash | no preset: a short white flash on the cut |\n\n## Rules\n- Pacing keeps the order of the user's shots and moves their cuts toward the reference's shot lengths, and onto beats when onBeatRatio is high. Say how many cuts moved.\n"
  },
  "remaking-videos/references/motivation.md": {
    "name": "motivation",
    "description": "Remakes a motivation or quote video: footage under a voiceover or quote, with bold captions.",
    "body": "# Remake: motivation video\n\nThe reference is footage under a voice (a speech, a quote, a narrator), with large captions that carry the words. A good remake gets three things right: the caption style, how each line is timed to the voice, and slow, weighty footage that cuts on the sentences.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Get the words from the user: their own voiceover, a speech they have the rights to, or a script they approve.\n2. Transcribe the voice (analyzeMedia transcribe) and key every caption and cut to its words.\n3. Rebuild the reference's caption style from its titles: size, weight, case, font class, position, and how words appear (word by word, a pop, one key word in a color). Apply it with addSubtitlesFromTranscript and styleCaptions, or with applyCaptionStyle.\n4. Cut the footage on sentence ends and strong words, keeping the reference's median shot length.\n5. Match the grade and texture (contrast, desaturation, grain, vignette).\n\n## Rules\n- Never reuse the reference's voiceover or its quote as the remake.\n- Speech counts only once the transcript has it: a video description (inspectVideo, understand) can invent a voiceover.\n- Keep the reference's line length (the number of words on screen at once).\n- Slow shots stay slow: use smoothSlowMotion or setPlaybackRate when the reference ran slowed footage.\n"
  },
  "remaking-videos/references/recreation.md": {
    "name": "recreation",
    "description": "The full recreation workflow: section inventories, exact content, layered camera behavior, rendered transition comparisons and close-out. Existing edits start with writing-scenes page edit-lanes.",
    "body": "# Recreating a motion film\n\n## Contents\n- Before starting\n- 1. Map the film, then inventory one section\n- 2. Read camera behavior per layer\n- 3. Build and verify this section before the next\n- 4. Close out against the inventory\n\n## Before starting\n- For edits to an existing recreation, start with writing-scenes page `edit-lanes`. The inventory, dense reads, comparisons and close-out below are the recreate lane's full checks.\n- The supplied video is the brief. Read launch-films-motions and writing-scenes for implementation. This page owns the recreation workflow.\n- Take the words, UI, brand, timing and motion from the reference; ask only for something the video cannot answer, such as a different format or length.\n- Rebuild graphics, UI and captions as editable layers; retain separately addressable footage where the requested workflow uses it. Do not flatten the remake into playback of the reference.\n- The measured recreation inventory governs the editable reconstruction. During the build, read `image-plates` for real asset needs; it sets what may be generated from a reference.\n\n## 1. Map the film, then inventory one section\n\n### Source format\n- Record the source width and height exactly, its frame rate (retain fractional rates such as 30000/1001), and video frame count.\n- Derive video duration from frame count divided by fps, not the audio tail.\n- Use those dimensions and timing for the requested remake; disclose any renderer limitation or requested format conversion.\n- Keep event times tied to source frame numbers to avoid rounding a switch onto the next frame.\n\n### Map the whole film\n- Read the whole reference with readFrames count 16 for its moments and holds.\n- Then run motionMap with its assetId across the whole reference in adjoining windows of up to 12 s (0–12, 12–24, …), before writing the inventory.\n- Every move motionMap returns is an inventory item; group these into named sections with their incoming and outgoing transitions.\n\n### Dense reads of a section\n- Before building a section, read every change from a little before its onset through its settle with readFrames at the reference's full source fps (from the readFrames result).\n- Each sheet holds at most 30 frames: shorten windows and continue from the last returned time until the entire section is read.\n- Inspect consecutive full-resolution frames on both sides of cuts, entrances, exits, flashes and caption switches; sparse sheets locate events but cannot establish their frame timing.\n- Open full-size frames or crops for details.\n\n### The effect inventory\nWrite a numbered effect inventory in your reply, with one item per visible change, including simultaneous changes on different layers:\n- Time window and what it is, including its first visible frame, end and settle.\n- Layer: background, foreground, cursor or UI; name the object within that layer.\n- Type: cut, continuous zoom, spring, stagger, morph, mask, blur, dither, click, hover, scatter or the specific behavior seen.\n- Count: every container, piece, click and staggered entry, including their order and spacing.\n- Color and thickness: measured colors, strokes and pixel/block sizes.\n- Exact content: every word, logo, icon, face and shape, plus its source crop.\n- Text: compare source and rendered visible ink bounds in source pixels (width, height, baseline and spacing), then weight, outline and shadow. Measure the caption band’s actual pixel switches independently of transcript word times; preserve hard switches unless a fade is visible.\n- Blur: record source frames proving smear direction, extent and duration. Rebuild directional motion blur on the moving layer only when observed; symmetric blur is not evidence of a directional match.\n\n### Build only from the inventory\n- A three-container menu needs three staggered containers.\n- Record scattered cubes, clicks and text hovers during scrolling even when the moments detector does not name them.\n- Crop and read every logo, icon, face and text before implementing it. Use findIcon for an identified icon, traceShape for its actual outline, matchFont for type and measureFrame for colors and edges.\n- Never substitute a guessed logo or symbol for a face or unread detail. Re-read anything uncertain.\n\n## 2. Read camera behavior per layer\n- Use trackReference separately on background and foreground objects that move differently; record what stays fixed, onset, path, acceleration, overshoot and settle.\n- Every tween uses the ease motionMap or trackReference measured, or a CustomEase built from it: for example, no default power2.out without a measurement behind it.\n- Keep a continuous push continuous across section boundaries and a measured cut instantaneous.\n\n## 3. Build and verify this section before the next\n\n### Build in sections\n- Use addFilm for one continuous film, written one section at a time in parts (writing-scenes page `films`). builtThrough tells addFilm and patchScene how far the motion reads must cover.\n- Correct a section, review its diff and record the fix with its evidence as writing-scenes page `edit-lanes` describes (Scenes and films).\n- A transition spanning sections belongs on the outer `tl` (shared code): include the affected sections in scope and verify both sides of the boundary.\n\n### Big moves first\n- Match the big moves first: every inventory transition, layer, count, order, onset, duration and camera behavior must match before polishing colors or offsets.\n- Do not close a button on the scene-change frame unless the reference does.\n\n### Verify rendered pixels\n- Verify rendered pixels, never code, tween parameters or cue times. An effect scheduled at the right time may remain invisible behind a threshold or mask.\n- Find its first visible rendered frame and compare it with the reference, then its pace, peak and disappearance.\n\n### compareReference on every transition\n- Run compareReference with start, end and fps 15 across every transition window.\n- Split long windows into adjoining calls and inspect every returned pair. Sparse times are only for steady stretches.\n- Crop a small effect or detail and score that region on its own; a quiet background can hide a missing click or wrong pixel thickness in a whole-frame score.\n\n### motionMap on the build\n- Run motionMap with assetId plus timelineStart on every window of up to 12 s: done means zero motion differences on the current revision.\n- Fix each listed difference with patchScene and recheck, or mark its window off (Refused or failing checks).\n- Inspect uncertain layers with trackReference and cropped comparisons; still-frame scores alone never certify motion.\n\n### Refused or failing checks\n- If a render or compare is refused (job limit, timeout), wait about 30 s and retry its exact nextAction.\n- A refused check is not a pass: never mark a section done or end the recreation because checks could not run.\n- After 2 motionMap failures, or for an unmeasurable effect, mark that window off with motionMap off:{time,reason} and carry the timed reason into close-out. Off never means matched.\n\n## 4. Close out against the inventory\n\n### Whole-film comparison\n- Run compareReference with start/end/fps 10 in adjoining windows across the whole film (up to 60 samples per call, about 6 s at 10 fps); it renders the clone beside the reference frame for frame.\n- Write down in plain words what still differs in rhythm, smoothness, entrances and exits, and fix it with patchScene.\n- Mark every numbered inventory item matched by rendered evidence or off with its time and reason, including every off item in the motionMap reports.\n- Compare the final rendered output on the saved revision against the source, including dimensions, duration, cut and caption timing, and changed motion windows.\n- Report what remains approximate, substituted fonts/assets, unverified intervals and the actual comparison results; claim only the agreement the evidence supports.\n- Recheck all section boundaries and motionMap windows after the final edits, then call finish.\n\n### Sound\n- Match music with matchMusic using the reference's tempo and main inventory moments.\n- Regenerate its effects with generateSound, place them at the matching visible hits and verify with checkSound.\n- End music and effects with the film. Never copy the reference audio. Where a matched track starts and ends follows skill `music`.\n- Confirm any voiceover with a transcript; if it cannot be reproduced, finish the visuals and state the missing recording explicitly.\n"
  },
  "remaking-videos/references/social-edit.md": {
    "name": "social-edit",
    "description": "Remakes an edit of footage cut to music (Instagram and TikTok edits, fan edits, velocity edits, sports and car edits).",
    "body": "# Remake: social edit\n\nThe reference is footage cut to a song. What makes it is the rhythm: where cuts fall on the beat, how speed ramps into hits, and which flash, shake or zoom marks each one. A good remake keeps that rhythm on the user's footage and the user's track.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Find, in the user's footage, the moments that do the same job as each reference shot: the reveal, the action, the slow beat before a drop. Use analyzeMedia understand, then inspectVideo asking for the actual moments needed.\n2. Measure the user's track with analyzeMusic and start it as skill `music` says.\n3. Place the shots with the lengths and moves in Defaults.\n4. Run alignCutsToBeat after placing the shots.\n5. Rebuild the titles and captions and match the look.\n\n## Defaults\n| Reference shot field | Remake |\n| --- | --- |\n| Length | the reference shot's length in beats (from rhythm.bpm), not in seconds; seconds only when the reference has no music |\n| transitionIn cut | a hard cut |\n| transitionIn, any other kind | the addTransition preset from page `borrow` (Transition presets), at the reference's measured length and direction |\n| Camera speed-ramp | setSpeedRamp into the hit |\n| Shake on a hit | a short shake effect at that beat |\n| Push-in | configureSmoothZoom or a zoom effect over the shot |\n| Titles and captions | their style (size, weight, case, enter) rebuilt with the text tools, with the user's words |\n| Look | the grade the reference describes (applyLut, setColorCurves, grain), on the user's footage |\n\n## Rules\n- Map the rhythm onto the user's beats (Defaults, Length): a cut that fell on a downbeat lands on a downbeat.\n"
  },
  "talking-head-graphics/SKILL.md": {
    "name": "talking-head-graphics",
    "description": "Sets the house look for graphics over footage: isometric line diagrams beside a talking head (near-black panels, outlined blocks, hatching, one accent edge, dimension lines), minimal captions and layout around the speaker, with a working example. Read before adding graphics or captions over footage; not for motion films made from scratch (launch-films-motions), and user direction and measured recreations override these defaults.",
    "body": "# Talking-head graphics\n\nA good result reads like a technical drawing that builds itself next to the speaker, in step with the words: ideas become outlined blocks, charts and measurements, never filled cartoons or icons. The face stays clear, one graphic is on screen at a time, and the captions stay readable around it.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Check scope: graphics and captions over footage only. Motion films made from scratch (launch, feature, teaser films) follow launch-films-motions and its pages instead; nothing in this skill applies to them.\n2. Check what overrides the house defaults (see Rules) before choosing colors and fonts.\n3. Turn the spoken idea into an isometric line diagram, the default. Choose a hold or motion that communicates the idea; page `example` demonstrates technique, not a required composition.\n4. Key each piece to its word in the transcript and build in reading order: base first, then what it carries, then the label, then the dimension line and note.\n5. Verify the rendered result with getFrames.\n\n## Defaults\n\n### Palette\n| Element | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Panel background | #0C0C0C; over footage, transparent with a dark gradient (transparent to rgba(0,0,0,.65 to .85)) |\n| Main edges | #D6D6D6 / #BDBDBD |\n| Axes and secondary posts | #777 |\n| Back edges and dimension lines | #888 |\n| Block faces | near-black fills, #111 to #202020 |\n| Hatching | #404040 to #555 |\n| Figures and labels | #F5F5F5 |\n| Kickers and axis labels | #A6A6A6 |\n| Notes | #858585 to #999 |\n| Accent | the subject's brand color when it has one (NVIDIA green #76B900 in the Jensen film), otherwise one muted hue |\n| Invented UI over footage | surfaces #111 to #202020, 1 px dividers, text #F5F5F5 / #A6A6A6, one accent hue per shot, sized for a 1080 frame |\n\n### Drawing\n| Element | Spec |\n| --- | --- |\n| Isometric block | top, front and side faces as separate paths, stroke 2.5, stroke-linejoin round |\n| Hatching | on one face only: an SVG pattern of 45 degree lines, 9 to 10 px apart, stroke width 1 |\n| Accent edge | one edge or line per graphic, for example the block's base, stroke 3 |\n| Support posts | stroke 2, from the upper block down to the lower one |\n| Dimension line | a thin line with end ticks (M x y H x2 plus short verticals), stroke 1.5, a small note below it |\n| Chart | axes stroke 1.5; values as 3 px round-capped lines; a small white dot (r 4 to 5) riding the line as it draws; an end label in mono |\n| Block label | Inter 25 to 27, weight 500, centered, at most 3 words |\n| Numbers | Geist Mono with tabular numerals |\n| Note | at most one per graphic, at most 6 words, 18 to 20 px |\n\n### Motion\n| Move | Spec |\n| --- | --- |\n| Lines draw on | DrawSVGPlugin, drawSVG 0% to 100%, power3.inOut, 0.6 to 1.2 s |\n| Blocks and labels reveal | left-to-right clipPath wipe, inset(0 100% 0 0) to inset(0), power3.inOut, about 0.65 s |\n| Stacked block | drops in from about 38 px above while its clip opens, power3.out, 0.8 s |\n| Panel change | wipe or slide, x 600 px, power3.inOut, 0.8 s |\n| Counter | power3.out, settling on the spoken value |\n\n### Captions\n| Property | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Type | Inter 36 px, weight 500, white |\n| Shadow | 0 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,.65) |\n| Placement | centered, no box, no color highlight |\n| Line | up to 3 words, broken at punctuation or pauses over 0.3 s |\n| Timing | each word appears at its spoken time |\n\n## Rules\n- Explicit user direction and measured literal references override the house defaults; confirmed brand fonts and colors override the remaining defaults. Why: copying a reference and inventing a film require different choices.\n- Keep diagram strokes flat, without glows. A requested grain gradient behind the diagram is allowed, and the footage legibility gradient is exempt.\n- No background grids or grid lines: they are clutter.\n- No scan lines or bright purple fills; block faces are never solid color.\n- Stacked blocks show a relationship (\"X carries Y\"): the upper block sits on thin support posts.\n- No headings or kickers, and never repeat the spoken words as titles on the graphic: they are already in the captions. Labels sit inside a block or at a line's end.\n- The speaker stays clear: graphics take the half of a split screen or the lower third, never cover the face.\n- One graphic at a time; the previous one leaves before the next idea starts.\n- Panels never change by fading everything out.\n- Let a completed graphic hold for recognition. Move a dot or count a value when it communicates change; avoid decorative drift.\n- Captions move out of the graphic's way (they ride with the layout when a split screen opens or closes) instead of being covered.\n\n## Example\nThe speaker says \"GeForce carries CUDA\" from 12.0 to 13.4 s, with the graphic in the lower third:\n- 12.0 s, \"GeForce\": the base block wipes in left to right (0.65 s, power3.inOut) while its accent base edge draws on; its label wipes in inside it.\n- 12.6 s, \"carries\": the support posts draw on and the CUDA block drops in from 38 px above (0.8 s, power3.out).\n- 13.4 s, \"CUDA\": its label wipes in, then the dimension line draws on. The graphic holds until the next idea; the captions stay centered below it.\n\nPage `example` has this as a complete scene module.\n\n## Pages\n- `example`: a complete stacked isometric diagram scene module, plus how to draw charts, bars, counters and structures the same way.\n"
  },
  "talking-head-graphics/references/example.md": {
    "name": "example",
    "description": "A complete scene module in the isometric line diagram style (stacked blocks, accent edge, dimension line, caption), plus how to draw charts, bars, counters and structures the same way.",
    "body": "# Example: a stacked isometric diagram\n\nA complete scene module in the isometric line diagram style: a base block draws on, a second block drops onto it on support posts, labels wipe in, a dimension line and its note (class `caption`) finish it, then it holds. This is an example, not a preset: choose shapes, values and timing for the idea.\n\n## Example\n\n```ts\nimport { DrawSVGPlugin } from 'gsap/DrawSVGPlugin';\nexport default function scene({ root, tl, gsap }) {\n  const ACCENT = '#76B900'; // the subject's brand color, or one muted hue\n  root.innerHTML = `<div style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;font-family:Inter\">\n  <svg class=\"dia\" width=\"528\" height=\"330\" viewBox=\"0 0 528 330\" style=\"position:absolute;left:696px;top:640px;overflow:visible\">\n    <defs><pattern id=\"hatch\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" patternUnits=\"userSpaceOnUse\"><path d=\"M0 10L10 0\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></pattern></defs>\n    <g class=\"base\">\n      <path d=\"M20 230L382 230L508 170L146 170Z\" fill=\"#181818\" stroke=\"#BDBDBD\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n      <path d=\"M20 230V270H382V230\" fill=\"url(#hatch)\" stroke=\"#BDBDBD\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n      <path d=\"M382 230L508 170V210L382 270\" fill=\"#151515\" stroke=\"#BDBDBD\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n    </g>\n    <path class=\"accent\" d=\"M20 270H382\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"${ACCENT}\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text class=\"baseLabel\" x=\"201\" y=\"258\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#F5F5F5\" font-size=\"27\" font-weight=\"500\">GeForce</text>\n    <path class=\"posts\" d=\"M110 160V230M350 160V230M450 112V196\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#777\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <g class=\"deck\">\n      <path d=\"M110 120H350L450 72H210Z\" fill=\"#202020\" stroke=\"#D6D6D6\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n      <path d=\"M110 120V160H350V120M350 160L450 112V72\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#D6D6D6\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n      <text x=\"230\" y=\"149\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#F5F5F5\" font-size=\"25\" font-weight=\"500\">CUDA</text>\n    </g>\n    <path class=\"dim\" d=\"M20 290H382M20 283V297M382 283V297\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text class=\"caption\" x=\"201\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#A6A6A6\" font-size=\"20\">GeForce carries CUDA.</text>\n  </svg></div>`;\n  const q = (s: string) => root.querySelector(s);\n  const draw = (t, at, d = .7) => tl.fromTo(t, { drawSVG: '0%' }, { drawSVG: '100%', duration: d, ease: 'power3.inOut' }, at);\n  const wipe = (t, at, d = .65) => tl.fromTo(t, { clipPath: 'inset(0% 100% 0% 0%)' }, { clipPath: 'inset(0% 0% 0% 0%)', duration: d, ease: 'power3.inOut' }, at);\n  // Times come from the transcript: each piece lands on its word.\n  wipe(q('.base'), .2, .85);\n  draw(q('.accent'), .2, .95);\n  draw(q('.posts'), .5, .8);\n  tl.fromTo(q('.deck'), { clipPath: 'inset(100% 0% 0% 0%)', y: -38 }, { clipPath: 'inset(0% 0% 0% 0%)', y: 0, duration: .8, ease: 'power3.out' }, .9);\n  wipe(q('.baseLabel'), 1.5, .55);\n  draw(q('.dim'), 1.8, 1.2);\n  wipe(q('.caption'), 2.2, .8);\n  // Hold the completed relationship so it can be read.\n}\n```\n\n## Other shapes in the same style\n| Shape | How |\n| --- | --- |\n| Line chart | axes `M56 12V212` and `M56 212H500` (#777, 1.5), no grid lines; the value as a 3 px round-capped path drawn with drawSVG while a white dot (r 5) follows it with MotionPathPlugin (`motionPath:{path, align: path, alignOrigin:[.5,.5]}`); the end value in Geist Mono |\n| Comparison bars | outlined rects filled with the hatch pattern, revealed with the left-to-right wipe and staggered .12 to .14 s; one accent bracket measures the biggest |\n| Counter | a Geist Mono number tweened on an object with onUpdate, power3.out, landing on the spoken value |\n| Structure / house | uprights and roof lines as separate paths drawn on in order (front edges #F5F5F5, back edges #888), so the drawing builds like a sketch |"
  },
  "transitions/SKILL.md": {
    "name": "transitions",
    "description": "How one footage shot becomes the next (reels, vlogs, clips, gaming and sports montages, any edit of real footage): the hard cut (the default), whips with addTransition (left, right, up, down, zoom, zoom-out; 4–5 frames), fades, when to whip and how many on beat-synced montages. Read before adding any transition or whip to footage.",
    "body": "# Transitions\n\nA good shot change is a hard cut, unless a chosen beat earns more. A whip is 4–5 frames: both shots stay composed where they are, the outgoing one flicks a short way in one direction with motion blur along that direction, and the incoming one arrives already moving the same way and settles. Nothing slides a full frame.\n\nFilms made from scratch whip with `whip()`: launch-films-motions page `shot-changes`.\n\nTerms: the **cut** is the first frame of the incoming shot; `f` is one frame (1/30 s at 30 fps); **travel** is how far the picture moves, as a share of the frame's width (left/right) or height (up/down).\n\n## Workflow\n1. Pick the kind for each cut from Defaults. Start from a hard cut; whip only the cuts When to whip allows.\n2. Add each whip or fade with addTransition across the cut (Settings).\n3. Check every whip with getFrames as Check lists.\n\n## Defaults\nAt 30 fps. The user's direction and a reference being copied win.\n\n| Shot change | Frames | Seconds | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hard cut | 0 | 0 | Every cut by default |\n| Whip (left, right, up, down, zoom, zoom-out) | 4–5 | 0.13–0.17 | A topic change, a chosen music hit, a montage beat |\n| Crossfade | 9–18 | 0.3–0.6, aim 0.5 | A change of time or mood only |\n| Fade to black | 9 | 0.3 | The end of the edit, or a jump in time (configureVisualFade) |\n\n### Whip settings\n| Whip | Travel | Outgoing shot | Incoming shot | Peak blur at 1080p |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Left, right | 15% of width (10–25%) | flicks 15% toward that side, `power4.in` | arrives 15% from the other side, `expo.out` | 24 px, horizontal only |\n| Up, down | 15% of height (10–25%) | flicks 15% toward that side, `power4.in` | arrives 15% from the other side, `expo.out` | 24 px, vertical only |\n| Zoom | scale 1.25 | scales 1 → 1.25, `power4.in` | scales 1.12 → 1, `expo.out` | 10 px |\n| Zoom-out | scale 0.8 | scales 1 → 0.8, `power4.in` | scales 0.88 → 1, `expo.out` | 10 px |\n\nThe direction is where the picture travels on screen: in a whip left, both shots move toward the left edge. The shot not in front fills the edge the moving one uncovers.\n\n### Frame by frame (whip left, 4 frames, cut at T)\n| Frame | In front | Offset | Blur |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T − 3f | outgoing, at rest | 0 | 0 |\n| T − 2f | outgoing | −0.9% | 1.5 px |\n| T − f | outgoing, peak | −15% | 24 px |\n| T | incoming, peak | +15% | 24 px |\n| T + f | incoming | +0.5% | 0.8 px |\n| T + 2f | incoming, at rest | 0 | 0 |\n\n## Rules\n\n### When to whip\n- **Hard cut by default.** Whip only on a topic change or a music hit you chose. Check: at most one cut in three is a whip, and never two whips in a row.\n- **Beat-synced montages** (gaming, sports, music edits whose cuts land on beats): up to every other cut may whip, still never two in a row. Example: 12 beat cuts, at most 6 whips, on the bigger hits.\n- **Never whip:**\n  - a calm, slow or emotional moment (hard cut, or a crossfade for a change of time or mood);\n  - a jump cut inside one talking-head sentence (hard cut);\n  - against the motion in the shots: a whip right after a shot that pans left;\n  - when a reference uses a different transition at that cut (match the reference).\n- Other presets (spin-zoom, film-burn, gradient-wipe, iris-wipe, blur-dissolve, zoom-through) only when the user asks for one or a reference uses one.\n\n### Settings\naddTransition is part of every footage lane (edits, reels, clips); film lanes use launch-films-motions page `shot-changes` instead.\n\n| Whip | addTransition |\n|---|---|\n| Left, right, up, down | `preset: \"whip-pan\"`, `whipDirection: \"left\"` (or `right`, `up`, `down`) |\n| Zoom | `preset: \"whip-pan\"`, `whipDirection: \"zoom\"` |\n| Zoom-out | `preset: \"whip-pan\"`, `whipDirection: \"zoom-out\"` |\n| All whips | `travel` 0.15 (0.1–0.25); `frames` 4 (default) or 5, 3 or 6 only to match a reference; `blur` 24 px (zooms 10), 0 for none |\n\n- Whips center on the cut: 4 frames is 2 before it and 2 after; 5 frames starts half a frame early.\n- whipDirection follows the motion in the shots: a shot panning right gets a whip right.\n- zoom-through is a push, not a whip: the outgoing shot blows past 4× and the incoming one grows in from 0.35×.\n\n## Check\n- getFrames rounds a time down to its frame, so ask 0.5 ms after each frame: for a cut at 6 s, 5.9675 (T − f), 6.0005 (T) and 6.0675 (T + 2f).\n- At T − f the outgoing shot is about 15% along the whip; at T the incoming one is about 15% behind, arriving the same way. Both are smeared along the whip axis only.\n- At T + 2f the incoming shot is sharp and in place.\n- Count the cuts: at most one in three is a whip (every other cut on a beat-synced montage), never two in a row.\n\n## Example\nA 40 s gaming montage cut on the music's beats, 12 cuts:\n- 6 whips on the biggest hits, never two in a row: `addTransition { fromClipId, toClipId, preset: \"whip-pan\", whipDirection: \"right\" }`, the direction following the crosshair's motion; a zoom on the drop: `whipDirection: \"zoom\"`.\n- The other 6 cuts stay hard cuts.\n- getFrames 0.5 ms after T − f and T for each whip shows the short, axis-smeared flick; T + 2f is sharp.\n"
  },
  "visual-inspection/SKILL.md": {
    "name": "visual-inspection",
    "description": "Finds and removes awkward moments the picture shows from normal footage (talking heads, vlogs, podcasts): dead air, a frozen face, looking away or at a script, reaching for the camera, fidgeting, jumps and flashes. Read before any visually motivated cut; for recreating a reference or measuring motion, read remaking-videos instead.",
    "body": "# Awkward moments in footage\n\nA good result removes only the moments the user named, each one confirmed in frames, with every cut on word boundaries so the speech stays whole. The reply says what was removed, by time, and what was looked at and kept.\n\nThis covers \"remove the awkward moments\", \"cut where I look away\", \"clean up the dead air\", script glances, camera fiddling, or any cut the picture motivates. The agent looks at the footage itself with readFrames: no guessing from the transcript, no Gemini needed.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Find candidates and merge them into one list of SOURCE windows (see Find candidates).\n2. Confirm each candidate frame by frame (see Confirm).\n3. Apply all cuts at once on the clip: cleanSpeech with keep (the SOURCE ranges between the cuts), or retainSourceRanges with tightenSilence:true. The transcript-only candidates (pauses, fillers) are handled in the same call. Close gaps if several clips are left.\n4. Verify with the checks writing-scenes page `edit-lanes` sets for the lane. In the reel lane, the source join sequences already read in step 1 count as the join evidence; do not render them again.\n5. Reject any join that lands on a frozen face or a half glance, and fix it.\n6. Tell the user what was removed, by time and what it was (\"0:12 looked down at notes, 0:31 reached for the camera, 4.2 s of dead air at the start\"), and what was looked at and kept.\n\n## Find candidates\n\n### Reel lane\n- Read one cached readFrames overview grid (count 12, stillsOnly:true) for orientation.\n- Confirm each proposed join with a short SOURCE sequence around the retained outgoing end and incoming start (about 0.3–0.5 s at 10 fps). Batch explicit times with stillsOnly:true.\n- Revisit only suspicious joins.\n- Do not run a whole-source motion map, and do not infer a look-up from isolated stills.\n\n### Other workflows\n- Transcript: inspectAssetAnalysis (analyzeMedia transcribe first if there is none). Note pauses over about 0.7 s, false starts and restarts, \"um/uh/so/like\" before a restart, and the words around each gap.\n- Picture: readFrames momentsOnly:true across the whole source (outside the reel lane, or in it when its full checks run). Read each moment with the table below.\n- Short clip: for a clip under about 20 s, readFrames count 16 over the whole clip may replace the moments map; it returns the moments plus a picture.\n- Skip a moment while the speaker is mid-sentence and calm: that is ordinary talking.\n- Strongest candidates: moments in or next to a transcript pause, at the very start (before the first word) and at the very end (after the last word).\n\n| Moment in the map | What it may be |\n| --- | --- |\n| Hold (a second or more where the picture all but stops) | During silence: dead air or a frozen face. A hold while speaking is kept. |\n| Small motion in the face or hand region (compare the region to where the face is) | Looking away, at notes or down at the phone, fidgeting, touching hair or face, reaching for the camera |\n| Big motion covering most of the frame | Standing up, walking out of frame, picking up or moving the camera |\n| Cut | A jump from an earlier rough cut, a flash, a frame drop |\n\n## Confirm\n- Reel-lane join sequences count as confirmation when clear; do not re-read them.\n- For any other candidate, readFrames fps 10 from about 0.5 s before it to about 0.5 s after; split a longer window into several reads. Several candidates close together can share one read.\n- Look at the grid and decide. Awkward:\n  - eyes leave the lens for 0.5 s or more during silence and return\n  - the face freezes or goes blank\n  - a hand comes up to the camera or face\n  - the head drops to read\n  - the camera shakes or is being set up\n  - a flash or a jump\n- Keep blinks, nods, talking gestures and glances under 0.5 s unless the user targeted them.\n- Bracket onset and recovery at 10 fps, then inspect the adjacent native-rate frames in each bracket for the final boundaries.\n- Never cut on the map alone: it says where the picture moves, not why.\n\n## Cut\n- Each cut runs from the frame the awkward thing starts to the frame the speaker is back, widened to the nearest word boundaries (word timings from inspectAssetAnalysis with start/end).\n- Never cut inside a word.\n- If the awkward moment happens while the speaker is talking, cut the whole sentence or phrase, or leave it and say so. Do not cut words out of a sentence to hide a glance.\n\n## Long clips\n- Other workflows confirm only the momentsOnly candidates; never page the entire clip at 10 fps.\n- Use inspectVideo only when the user asks about something that must be heard (laughing, a cough, a phone ringing) or for a search by meaning across a long clip.\n- inspectFrames is not needed when readFrames can show the frames.\n\n## Rules\n- Only cut what the user asked about. \"Clean it up\" with no visual words is a speech cleanup (cleanSpeech), not this skill.\n- An explicit transcript-only request skips visual inspection.\n- A quiet pause alone is not an awkward moment.\n- If the frames leave it unclear, keep the footage and say what was uncertain. Never claim a moment was removed that was not seen.\n\n## Example\n\"Cut where I look away\" on a 2-minute vlog, outside the reel lane:\n1. readFrames momentsOnly:true finds small motion in the face region at 0:12 and 0:31.\n2. readFrames fps 10 from 0:11.5 to 0:13.5: the head drops to read notes from 0:12.2 to 0:13.1, during a pause. At 0:31 it is a nod.\n3. The cut runs 0:12.2 to 0:13.1, widened to the word boundaries around the pause, applied with cleanSpeech keep.\n4. Reply: \"Removed 0:12, looked down at notes (0.9 s). Looked at 0:31, a nod, and kept it.\"\n"
  },
  "writing-scenes/SKILL.md": {
    "name": "writing-scenes",
    "description": "Defines the plain HTML + GSAP module contract that scene clips and films share: the scene() arguments, what loads, fonts, images, project media and limits. Read before the first addFilm, addScene or editScene, together with launch-films-motions for how a film should look and move.",
    "body": "# Writing scenes\n\nA scene is a plain TypeScript module that builds HTML once and choreographs it on a GSAP timeline. It renders full-frame on its own V layer, inside a sandbox, frame by frame for preview and export; transparent areas show the layers below.\n\nA good scene follows the contract below exactly, so preview, scrubbing and export show the same frames. A film is one such module placed as one clip with addFilm, its sections written as `section(...)` calls (page `films`).\n\n## Workflow\n1. **Existing scene or film:** start with page `edit-lanes` and follow it instead of the steps below.\n2. **Read the brief** with readBrief and follow it. Save lasting direction with updateBrief.\n3. **Plan** a short outline: a few lines per section with its time range, what moves and the words.\n4. **Write** the code directly in the addFilm or addScene call (Rules, Writing).\n5. **Check** the rendered frames as launch-films-motions \"Before handing back\" lists, then call finish.\n\n## Rules\n\n### Writing\n- Never draft scene code while thinking. A turn has one output budget for thinking and the call together, and a long draft in thinking leaves nothing for the scene.\n- A film: addFilm, as page `films` describes.\n- A standalone scene (title card, overlay): addScene, one at a time, checking each before writing the next. If several are simple and independent, addScenes saves them together.\n\n### Contract\n- Write `export default function scene({ root, tl, gsap, width, height, duration, fps, brand, media, icon, blueprint, section, shot, push, hit, cursor, beat, downbeat, accent, beats, reveal, exit, swap, counter, type, mark, whip })`. It runs once, before the first frame.\n- `root` is the full-frame container on the 1080-based canvas (`width` x `height` px). Build inside it with `root.innerHTML` or `document.createElement`, positioned with absolute px or percentages.\n- Never restyle `root` itself.\n- `tl` is the scene's GSAP timeline. Time 0 is the clip's first frame, in seconds. How moves go on it: gsap page `revline`.\n- `section(name, start, duration, build)` marks a film section (page `films`).\n- `shot`, `push`, `hit`, `cursor` and `beat`/`downbeat`/`accent`/`beats` are motion helpers; API reference in page `films`, Motion helpers.\n- `reveal`, `exit`, `swap`, `counter`, `type` and `mark` are text functions; API reference in the gsap skill, Text functions.\n- `whip(tl, { out, in, at, direction })` whips from one shot to the next (launch-films-motions page `shot-changes`).\n- `media` holds the project images and videos attached by name (Project media).\n- `icon('scissors', { size: 16, color: '#8d8f93', strokeWidth: 2 })` returns a Lucide icon as SVG markup (kebab-case names, as on lucide.dev). Use it for every UI icon instead of drawing one.\n- `blueprint('Editor', container, { width, height })` builds a brand kit blueprint attached with `blueprints` inside `container` and returns its root; animate its `[data-part=...]` pieces.\n- `brand` is the brand kit attached with brandKitId, or null. Its fields: launch-films-motions page `brand-kit`.\n- Scenes are plain TypeScript, never JSX or React.\n- Imports: `gsap` and its plugins ({{revline:0}}, plus EasePack), each from `gsap/<Name>` or all from `gsap`. Nothing else loads: no network, no other packages.\n- Limits: code up to {{revline:3}} characters per scene and {{revline:4}} per timeline (code shared by several clips counts once), scene clips up to {{revline:5}} s, films up to {{revline:6}} s. finish checks them.\n\n### Fonts and images\n- `font-family` may be any of: {{revline:1}}, or the brand kit's families when one is attached.\n- Images come from `media` or are inline SVG or data: URIs; nothing loads from the network.\n\n### Project media\n- Use the project's media: the user adds files in the editor; over MCP, uploadMedia adds them and getProject lists them. Images (png, jpg, webp) and videos up to 2 minutes.\n- Attach them by name: addScene or editScene with `media: [{ name: 'editor', assetId: '…' }]`.\n- In code, `media.editor` is a URL: `<img src=\"${media.editor}\">`, `<video src=\"${media.demo}\">` or `background-image:url(${media.editor})`.\n- `media.<name>` is undefined when the file is missing, so keep a fallback.\n- Videos follow the scene clock, frame-exact, and are always muted (sound belongs on audio clips). Never call play(); the engine seeks them.\n- Video attributes: `data-start` is the scene second the video begins (default 0), `data-offset` the source second it starts from, `data-rate` its speed, `data-loop` loops it.\n- Treat media like any element: crop it with a wrapper and `overflow:hidden`, zoom with scale and transform-origin, tilt with perspective, mask, blur, stack copies.\n- Media is loaded before the first frame, in preview and in exports.\n\n## Example\n\n```ts\n{{revline:2}}```\n\n## Pages\n- `edit-lanes`: start here for any edit to existing footage, captions, scenes or films.\n- `films`: how a film is written as one module with `section(...)` calls, built in parts and edited.\n"
  },
  "writing-scenes/references/edit-lanes.md": {
    "name": "edit-lanes",
    "description": "Sets which checks each lane runs for edits to existing footage, captions, scenes or films, and the smallest read-edit-verify path for each. Read first for any such edit, before reading a transcript or code.",
    "body": "# Editing existing footage, captions, scenes or films\n\nA good edit changes only the requested range, keeps everything around it in sync, and is verified at exactly the moments it touched. The lane sets the checks.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Pick the checks for this edit from Rules, Which checks.\n2. Read only the affected range: the transcript window for footage and captions, the affected section for a film.\n3. Make the edit in as few calls as possible (Rules, Footage and captions or Scenes and films).\n4. Verify at each changed moment, then report what was sampled and anything that had to move.\n\n## Rules\n\n### Direction and feedback\n- **Exact direction from the user:** do exactly that. Never override the user.\n- **Approved work is off-limits:** change it only when the user names it. When the user approves a section, mark it with approveClip.\n- **Feedback on one part:** change only that part. Every other section, clip, title, timing, move, color and sound stays exactly as it was.\n- Never \"improve\" what was not asked about. If a change forces something else to shift (later timings, an audio cue), keep it otherwise identical and say what moved.\n- **Back to what we had** (\"go back to what we had\", \"it was better before\"): listRevisions, look with getFrames revision, then restoreSection for one section (restoreClip for one clip, restoreTimeline for the whole tab). Never re-patch by hand to recreate it, and never chain undoLatestEdit.\n\n### Which checks\n- A final export or publish request, or an explicit request to double-check everything, runs the lane's full checks. The server decides that from the request.\n- **New scene, film or recreation:** the motion or recreate lane, which always runs full checks. Keep check metadata in tool results.\n- **Plain talking-head speech cleanup in the reel lane:** saved-data checks; sample new or changed caption styling or framing once.\n- That reel path needs no frames at every speech cut and no compulsory listening. Explicit visual requests and uncertain joins still get targeted checks.\n- **Other existing static, text or asset change, or a named speech cut:** local checks in every lane.\n- **Existing motion, timing, camera or transition edit:** local checks in the edit lane; full changed-range checks in the motion and recreate lanes and for final checks.\n- Story restructuring checks every changed spot. An explicit whole-film review is a full double-check.\n\n### Local and full checks\n- **Local checks:** getFrames at each changed moment and one frame either side (`t - 1/fps`, `t`, `t + 1/fps`, within the timeline).\n- Local checks skip full sweeps, reference compares, repeated finishing passes, compareReference, motionMap and checkPacing.\n- **Full checks:** launch-films-motions \"Before handing back\" for films and scenes and, for copies, the close-out in remaking-videos page `recreation`; finish adds the automatic timing and limit checks. When scene motion changed, follow the full lane.\n- Rendered checks are required for visual changes in every lane, except the plain reel cleanup above.\n- Reuse loaded skills and project context.\n\n### Footage and captions\n- Transcript reads and speech cuts follow footage-editing page `speech`.\n- Ripple the actually removed timeline intervals across affected tracks so captions, b-roll and linked audio stay in sync: trim or delete intersecting material, then batch moveClips by the shared time delta.\n- Verify with inspectTimeline: the new duration meets the target, with no gaps or orphaned captions.\n\n### Scenes and films\n- Find code with inspectClip search, then read only the affected `section` (or `lines` when there are no sections).\n- Make one patchScene call where possible, with that section and expectedRevision.\n- Never rebuild a film section as a separate scene clip.\n- Review diffScene for edits outside the requested scope. After a shared-code edit, check the other sections it touches and the adjacent boundaries.\n- Report the sampled scope, never whole-film visual equivalence.\n- Attach the actual verification evidence with recordFix.\n"
  },
  "writing-scenes/references/films.md": {
    "name": "films",
    "description": "Explains how a film is written as one plain GSAP module with section() calls: what each section owns, where shared code goes, and how a long film is written in parts, plus the API reference for the motion helpers (shot, push, beat, cursor, hit). Read before addFilm and before editing a film's code.",
    "body": "# Films\n\nA good film module builds its whole DOM once, keeps each section's motion inside its own `section(...)` call, and runs on one continuous timeline, so sections can be read, edited and approved one at a time. It uses the same contract as any scene (SKILL.md, Contract).\n\n## Workflow\n1. Build the whole DOM up front in the default function.\n2. Put each section's moves inside its `section(...)` call, and helpers only one section uses at top level.\n3. Pass addFilm the film's `sections` (title and start second each) matching the calls.\n4. For a long film, write it in parts (Rules, Long films).\n5. Edit it afterwards with inspectClip and patchScene, one section at a time.\n\n## Rules\n\n### One film, one clip\n- `tl` counts film seconds from the first frame; `duration` is the whole film.\n- Sections are contiguous: each one starts where the previous ends.\n- Split a film into several clips only when the user asks.\n\n### Sections\n- `section(name, start, duration, build)` places a child timeline on the film timeline at `start` and passes it to `build`.\n- Positions inside it are section-local seconds (0 = section start). The runtime throws if a section's moves run past its `duration`.\n- `start` and `duration` are number literals: the engine reads them from the source text.\n- A whip between two sections: the `whip(tl, { out, in, at, direction })` helper (launch-films-motions page `shot-changes`); it may straddle a section's end.\n- Camera elements carry `data-camera` or have \"cam\" in their selector, variable or id: that is how finish tells the camera, the only element allowed moves over 1 s, from the rest.\n- Keep each section's look in its own call and helpers, so shared edits stay rare.\n\n### Long films\n- Write a long film in parts as addFilm describes: a marker comment, builtThrough, then patchScene insert for each later part.\n- A later part that needs new elements creates them inside its own `section(...)` call (appending to the stage), so the shared code stays untouched.\n- Never retype what is already there.\n\n## Motion helpers\nAPI reference for the helpers a scene receives next to `section`. Each one owns its mechanics (eases, overlaps, decay, guards), so a film only chooses properties. Every option has a default; numbers outside a range are clamped to it. All of them are seek-safe: frame N draws the same picture in preview and export.\n\n### shot\n`shot(name, start, duration, options, build)` is a `section` that is its own shot: its own container, its own camera, shown only from `start` to `start + duration`. A film made of shots is separate shots joined by cuts or whips, not one continuous camera world. `start` and `duration` are number literals, as for `section`. `build(tl, shot)` gets the section timeline and `{ name, el, start, duration, end }`.\n- `el`: the shot's container (selector or element). Default: the element with `data-shot=\"<name>\"`. Its children move into a camera layer, `shot.camera` (`data-camera`), which `push` moves. Never transform the container or the camera yourself; animate what is inside. The container's own layout (flex, grid centering) does not reach its children once they are in the camera layer: position each child itself (`position:absolute;inset:0;display:grid;place-items:center` to center one).\n- The cut lands on the frame nearest `start`, so a literal rounded to milliseconds still cuts on the marker's frame.\n- `enter.from`: `'left'`, `'right'`, `'up'`, `'down'`, `'zoom'` or `'none'` (default). The entrance is already moving on the shot's first frame: it began `overlap` frames before the cut, so nothing lands on a frozen frame. A hold after it settles is fine.\n- `enter.overlap`: frames, default 4, 0 to 12 (and under `frames`).\n- `enter.frames`: the whole entrance, default 14, 6 to 40.\n- `enter.distance`: fraction of the frame it travels, default .3, .05 to 1 (`'zoom'` starts at scale 1 + distance / 2).\n- `enter.ease`: `'expo'` (default) or `'power4'`, always `.out`.\n- `exit`: `'cut'` (default) or `'whip'`: the last 5 frames accelerate out (power4.in), away from the side the next shot enters from. `{ type: 'whip', to: 'left', frames: 5 }` names the side (frames 2 to 12).\n- `on`: `'beat'`, `'downbeat'` or `'accent'`: throws unless `start` is on a music marker of that kind (within half a frame), naming the nearest one.\n```ts\nshot('Editor', 2.4, 3, { enter: { from: 'right' }, exit: 'whip', on: 'downbeat' }, (s, shot) => {\n  push(s, { target: '.timeline', amount: .12, at: .5 });\n});\n```\n\n### push\n`push(tl, { target, amount, frames, ease, at, center, camera })` moves the camera toward `target`: fast start, slow end.\n- `target`: the element it moves toward (required).\n- `amount`: how much closer, default .15 (15%), .04 to 1.5. Under .04 throws: a tiny tail push (97% to 100%) reads as a stutter.\n- `frames`: default 0.8 s of frames, 6 to 4 s.\n- `ease`: `'expo'` (default) or `'power4'`, always `.out`; never inOut.\n- `at`: start, in seconds of `tl`, default 0. A negative `at` throws (usually film seconds on a section's timeline).\n- `center`: how far the target moves toward the frame center, default 1, 0 (zoom in place) to 1. The camera never shows the shot's edge.\n- `camera`: default the current shot's `el`; required outside a shot.\n- A push that overlaps another push on the same camera throws (no double zoom). Add pushes on one camera in time order; each starts where the last one ended.\n```ts\npush(s, { target: '.cta', amount: .2, frames: 24, at: 1.1 });\n```\n\n### beat, downbeat, accent, beats\n`beat(n)`, `downbeat(n)` and `accent(n)` return the film second of the n-th (1-based) music marker of that kind; `beats(kind)` returns all of them (`'beats'` default, `'downbeats'`, `'accents'`). They read the markers markMusicBeats put on the timeline, mapped to this film's own clock. Run markMusicBeats on the song's clip with the kind needed first; with no markers of that kind, or an n past the last one, they throw and list what is there.\n- Film seconds: on a section's timeline subtract its start, `at: beat(9) - shot.start`.\n- A `section` or `shot` may also be timed by markers: `start` as `downbeat(2)`, `duration` as `downbeat(4) - downbeat(2)` (or a marker minus a number). Saving writes the number literals the engine reads, start floored to the millisecond so the cut stays on the marker's frame, plus a comment naming what was written: `shot('Editor', 2.866, 3.566 /* downbeat(2), downbeat(4) - downbeat(2) */, …)`. addFilm and patchScene return them as `beatTiming`; later edits find the literals. A `sections` entry with the same title takes that start.\n```ts\nhit(s, { at: accent(2) - shot.start, intensity: .6 });\n```\n\n### cursor\n`cursor(tl, { path, at, clicks, frames, pause, arc, size, color, parent, type })` draws a pointer that moves along `path` on a natural curve (eases in, slight arc, decelerates into each point), clicks with a press and a ripple, and can type into a field. Returns `{ el, end, arrivals }`: the cursor element, the `tl` second it finishes, and when it reaches each point.\n- `path`: targets (selectors or elements, aimed at their center) or `{ x, y }` px in `parent` (required). It appears at the first point at `at`.\n- `at`: default 0. `clicks`: `'last'` (default), `'all'`, `'none'` or path indexes like `[1, 3]`.\n- `frames`: frames per move, default from distance (10 frames to 1.4 s), 6 to 3 s. `pause`: frames after each click, default 6, 0 to 60.\n- `arc`: bow of each move as a fraction of its length, default .12, 0 to .4. `size`: px, default 30, 14 to 80. `color`: default `'#ffffff'`.\n- `parent`: default the current shot's camera, so a push carries the pointer with the UI; else `root`.\n- `type: { el, text, cps, caret }` types `text` into `el` after the last move and click: `cps` characters per second, default 14, 4 to 40; the caret stays solid while typing and blinks for `caret` seconds after, default 1, 0 to 10. The field's own content is replaced when typing starts.\n```ts\nconst pointer = cursor(s, { path: [{ x: 1400, y: 820 }, '.prompt'], at: .3, type: { el: '.prompt-text', text: 'Make it cinematic' } });\n```\n\n### hit\n`hit(tl, { at, intensity, frames, axis, flash, punch, target })` is an impact: a shake that is hardest on the frame at `at` and decays to rest exactly `frames` later, an optional one-frame flash and a punch-in scale.\n- `at`: default 0. `intensity`: default .5, 0 to 1 (1 moves 3.5% of the frame). `frames`: default 10, 3 to 30.\n- `axis`: `'both'` (default, with a slight rotation), `'x'` or `'y'`.\n- `flash`: `false` (default), `true` (white) or a CSS color, for exactly one frame.\n- `punch`: extra scale at the hit, default .04, 0 to .25, settling with the shake.\n- `target`: default the current shot's `el`, else `root`. On those, the scale also covers the shake so no edge shows. Another target must not have GSAP transforms of its own (x, y, scale, rotation): GSAP clears the CSS translate the shake uses.\n```ts\nhit(s, { at: 1.6, intensity: .7, flash: true });\n```\n\n## Example\n```ts\nimport { SplitText } from 'gsap/SplitText';\n\nexport default function scene({ root, tl, duration, section }) {\n  root.innerHTML = `\n    <div class=\"stage\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;background:#141413;color:#F4F4F0\">\n      <h1 class=\"title\" style=\"position:absolute;left:10%;top:40%;margin:0;font-size:112px\">Ship faster</h1>\n      <div class=\"card\" style=\"position:absolute;left:20%;top:30%;width:60%;height:40%;border-radius:24px;background:#2a2a2a\"></div>\n    </div>`;\n  const title = root.querySelector('.title');\n  const card = root.querySelector('.card');\n\n  tl.to(root.querySelector('.stage'), { scale: 1.04, duration, ease: 'none' }, 0);   // shared: spans every section\n\n  section('Hook', 0, 3, (s) => {\n    s.from(new SplitText(title, { type: 'words' }).words, { yPercent: 60, opacity: 0, duration: .45, ease: 'power3.out', stagger: .06 }, .2)\n      .to(title, { y: -40, opacity: 0, duration: .3, ease: 'power2.in' }, 2.6);\n  });\n\n  section('Product', 3, 4, (s) => {\n    s.from(card, { y: 60, scale: .96, opacity: 0, duration: .4, ease: 'power3.out' }, 0);   // from() hides it from the first frame\n  });\n\n  // --- more sections\n}\n```\n"
  }
};
